// The language pages
// ==================
// What `hive agents` writes into `.hivedocs/` about the language itself: one short
// page per feature, and an index in front of them.
//
// They are short on purpose. A page is read into an agent's context whole, so one
// page per feature — complete about its own and silent about the rest — is what
// lets somebody read only what they are touching.
//
// They are exact on purpose too: what an agent cannot read, it guesses, and a guess
// about a language this strict compiles nowhere. Every rule here is one the compiler
// enforces, and the source of each is the chapter of `spec/` named at the foot of
// the page. When the spec changes, these change with it.
import ./page

// Every page about the language. `library.pages()` and `ui.pages()` are the
// other halves, and `agents.hive` writes the three of them out together.
func pages(): page.Page[dyn] {
    return [
        page.Page("README.md", index()),
        page.Page("syntax.md", syntax()),
        page.Page("types.md", types()),
        page.Page("declarations.md", declarations()),
        page.Page("expressions.md", expressions()),
        page.Page("statements.md", statements()),
        page.Page("patterns.md", patterns()),
        page.Page("mutability.md", mutability()),
        page.Page("concurrency.md", concurrency()),
        page.Page("build-time.md", buildTime()),
        page.Page("bounds.md", proofs()),
        page.Page("generics.md", generics()),
        page.Page("modules.md", modules()),
        page.Page("builtins.md", builtins()),
        page.Page("tables.md", tables()),
        page.Page("queries.md", queries()),
        page.Page("testing.md", testing()),
        page.Page("laws.md", laws()),
        page.Page("cli.md", cli()),
        page.Page("lsp.md", lsp()),
        page.Page("pitfalls.md", pitfalls())
    ]
}

// --- The index ---------------------------------------------------------------

func index(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Hive, for whoever is writing it

        Hive is a memory-managed language that compiles to Go. Files end in ~BT~.hive~BT~; the
        standard library is ~BT~hive.*~BT~. Every vector index, match branch and SQL result
        column is checked at compile time.

        **Read the page for what you are about to write** instead of guessing the
        syntax; every refusal on these pages is a compile error. New to Hive: read
        [pitfalls](pitfalls.md) first.

        **If you have a language-server tool, ask it before reading a file**: ~BT~hive lsp~BT~
        answers what a name is and where it is declared in one line, where the file
        costs the whole file. See [lsp](lsp.md).

        | page | what is on it |
        | --- | --- |
        | [syntax](syntax.md) | comments, keywords, how a name must be spelled, both string forms |
        | [types](types.md) | scalars, vectors, ~BT~Result~BT~, declared types, maps, what may be assigned to what |
        | [declarations](declarations.md) | ~BT~type~BT~ ~BT~func~BT~ ~BT~proc~BT~ ~BT~query~BT~ ~BT~test~BT~ ~BT~law~BT~, and returning on every path |
        | [expressions](expressions.md) | operators, precedence, calls, named arguments, function values |
        | [statements](statements.md) | bindings, assignment, ~BT~if~BT~, ~BT~switch~BT~, loops, ~BT~echo~BT~ / ~BT~assert~BT~ / ~BT~panic~BT~ |
        | [patterns](patterns.md) | ~BT~is~BT~, its four pattern kinds, nesting them, narrowing, exhaustiveness, a ~BT~switch~BT~'s coverage |
        | [mutability](mutability.md) | ~BT~mut~BT~, ~BT~mut~BT~ parameters, why a binding sometimes copies |
        | [concurrency](concurrency.md) | ~BT~async~BT~, the await-all, ~BT~with timeout~BT~ |
        | [build-time](build-time.md) | ~BT~with compiler~BT~: a call run while the program builds, its answer built in |
        | [bounds](bounds.md) | how every index and slice is proved in range |
        | [generics](generics.md) | type variables and monomorphization |
        | [modules](modules.md) | ~BT~import~BT~, the four path kinds, importing Go, the library's short names |
        | [builtins](builtins.md) | the functions that need no import |
        | [tables](tables.md) | CSV, spreadsheets, and the ~BT~Table~BT~ type |
        | [queries](queries.md) | ~BT~query~BT~ declarations and the SQL in them |
        | [testing](testing.md) | ~BT~test~BT~, ~BT~assert~BT~, coverage |
        | [laws](laws.md) | ~BT~law~BT~: claims the compiler proves for every value, what a law may reach, a type's laws and a table's |
        | [cli](cli.md) | every ~BT~hive~BT~ command |
        | [lsp](lsp.md) | the language server: what to ask it instead of reading files, and how to connect it |
        | [pitfalls](pitfalls.md) | **the mistakes that do not compile** — read this one first |
        | [stdlib/](stdlib/README.md) | every ~BT~hive.*~BT~ module, one page each |

        ## Eight rules that catch everybody

        1. **There is no truthiness.** A condition is a ~BT~Bool~BT~ and nothing else.
        2. **~BT~!~BT~ inverts a ~BT~Bool~BT~ and nothing else**, and it binds tight: ~BT~!a && b~BT~ is
           ~BT~(!a) && b~BT~, and a match may not be negated at all.
        3. **Every index must be proved in range** at compile time, or guarded with
           ~BT~if v bounds i~BT~. See [bounds](bounds.md).
        4. **A slice's high bound is inclusive**: ~BT~v[1:3]~BT~ is three elements.
        5. **~BT~append~BT~ needs a ~BT~mut T[dyn]~BT~**, and ~BT~[dyn]~BT~ is declared, never inferred:
           ~BT~mut v := ["a"]~BT~ is a ~BT~Str[1]~BT~ and cannot grow.
        6. **No number is widened for you.** ~BT~Int~BT~ does not become ~BT~Float~BT~; go through
           ~BT~hive.conv.itf~BT~.
        7. **A name's spelling says what it is** — ~BT~camelCase~BT~ values, ~BT~PascalCase~BT~
           types — and it is enforced. ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~ is *allowed* for an immutable
           variable, never required: ~BT~camelCase~BT~ is the default for every variable.
        8. **Every call blocks its caller.** Concurrency is decided where a call is
           written, never where it is declared.

        Each page is condensed from ~BT~spec/~BT~ in the Hive repository (the normative
        reference) and ends with its source chapters; read those when a page leaves a
        question open.
    `)
}

// --- The language ------------------------------------------------------------

func syntax(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Syntax

        Files are UTF-8 ~BT~.hive~BT~, with no header. **Not** indentation-sensitive; a newline
        ends no statement; a lone ~BT~;~BT~ is skipped. Break a long expression before an
        operator, but a line opening with ~BT~(~BT~, ~BT~[~BT~ or ~BT~-~BT~ starts a new statement (inside
        brackets a leading ~BT~-~BT~ carries on). To break a subtraction elsewhere, end the
        line with the ~BT~-~BT~.

        ~CODE~hive
        // A comment runs to the end of the line. There is no block comment.
        x := 1 // ...or takes the tail of one.
        ~CODE~

        ## Keywords

        ~CODE~
        import   proc     func     query    test     type
        if       else     return   is       with     switch
        void     true     false    echo     assert   panic
        dyn      mut      async    await    for      in
        each     bounds   break    continue law      axiom
        ~CODE~

        - Only the lower-case spelling is reserved: ~BT~Return~BT~, ~BT~Query~BT~, ~BT~proC~BT~ are
          ordinary names. A field cannot be called ~BT~type~BT~.
        - ~BT~axiom~BT~ is reserved: any use is a compile error.
        - ~BT~as~BT~ (after an import path), ~BT~timeout~BT~ (~BT~with timeout~BT~), ~BT~compiler~BT~
          (~BT~with compiler~BT~) and ~BT~table~BT~ (a law's ~BT~for table~BT~) are keywords only there,
          ordinary names elsewhere.

        ## Name shapes (enforced)

        | shape | spelling | names |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~camelCase~BT~ | lower-case letter, then ASCII letters and digits | variables, parameters, fields, every ~BT~proc~BT~/~BT~func~BT~/~BT~query~BT~, an import's name |
        | ~BT~PascalCase~BT~ | upper-case letter, then ASCII letters and digits | types, their variants, and atoms |
        | ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~ | upper-case letter, then upper-case letters, digits, ~BT~_~BT~ | **also** allowed for a variable nothing reassigns — a constant |

        - **~BT~camelCase~BT~ is the default for every variable**; ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~ is optional,
          never required. Spelling never makes a binding immutable; only omitting ~BT~mut~BT~
          does.
        - Never ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~: a ~BT~mut~BT~ variable, a counting loop's counter, a parameter,
          field, callable, import name, or a name bound by a pattern or ~BT~for each~BT~.
        - Any name may open with one ~BT~_~BT~ (~BT~_scratch~BT~, ~BT~_MAX~BT~), the rest keeping its
          shape. ~BT~_~BT~ alone throws a value away.

        ## Literals

        - ~BT~42~BT~ is an ~BT~Int~BT~, 64-bit signed; ~BT~-1~BT~ is unary minus on ~BT~1~BT~.
        - ~BT~0xFF~BT~, ~BT~0b1010~BT~, ~BT~1_000_000~BT~. Hex and binary literals are the 64 bits they
          spell: ~BT~0xFFFF_FFFF_FFFF_FFFF~BT~ is a ~BT~UInt64~BT~.
        - ~BT~1.0~BT~ is a ~BT~Float~BT~; a leading or trailing dot is **not** a float.
        - ~BT~true~BT~/~BT~false~BT~ are ~BT~Bool~BT~. ~BT~#Ready~BT~ is an ~BT~Atom~BT~, PascalCase only. ~BT~[a, b, c]~BT~
          is a vector.

        ## Strings

        **~BT~"..."~BT~** takes escapes and ~BT~\{expression}~BT~ interpolation; a newline inside is
        kept. A backtick is plain text in one: ~BT~"~BT~ + a backtick + ~BT~"~BT~ is how to write it.

        | escape | means |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~\n~BT~ ~BT~\t~BT~ ~BT~\r~BT~ | newline, tab, carriage return |
        | ~BT~\"~BT~ ~BT~\\~BT~ | a quote, a backslash |
        | ~BT~\\{~BT~ | a literal ~BT~\{~BT~, not an interpolation |
        | ~BT~\X~BT~ | any other ~BT~X~BT~ is itself |

        **A backtick string** takes ~BT~\{expression}~BT~ interpolation; its only escape is
        ~BT~\\{~BT~ (any other ~BT~\~BT~ is itself). It may span lines and **cannot contain a
        backtick**. It is dedented at compile time, before interpolation: leading and
        trailing blank lines dropped, then the longest common leading whitespace of the
        non-blank lines (a tab and a space each count as one).

        ~CODE~hive
        func goSource(name: Str): Str \{
        	return ~BT~
        		package \{name}

        		func Assert(ok bool) \\{ }
        	~BT~   // for "main", opens at column 1: "package main\n\nfunc Assert(ok bool) \{ }"
        }
        ~CODE~

        ## Operator tokens

        | | |
        | --- | --- |
        | three-character | ~BT~...~BT~ (the vector-pattern rest marker), ~BT~<<=~BT~ ~BT~>>=~BT~, ~BT~<<<~BT~ ~BT~>>>~BT~ (the [binary](patterns.md) brackets) |
        | two-character | ~BT~:=~BT~ ~BT~>=~BT~ ~BT~<=~BT~ ~BT~==~BT~ ~BT~!=~BT~ ~BT~**~BT~ ~BT~&&~BT~ ~BT~\|\|~BT~ ~BT~++~BT~ ~BT~--~BT~ ~BT~+=~BT~ ~BT~-=~BT~ ~BT~*=~BT~ ~BT~/=~BT~ ~BT~&=~BT~ ~BT~\|=~BT~ ~BT~^=~BT~ ~BT~<<~BT~ ~BT~>>~BT~ ~BT~\|>~BT~ |
        | one-character | ~BT~\{~BT~ ~BT~}~BT~ ~BT~(~BT~ ~BT~)~BT~ ~BT~[~BT~ ~BT~]~BT~ ~BT~:~BT~ ~BT~;~BT~ ~BT~,~BT~ ~BT~.~BT~ ~BT~>~BT~ ~BT~<~BT~ ~BT~=~BT~ ~BT~+~BT~ ~BT~-~BT~ ~BT~*~BT~ ~BT~/~BT~ ~BT~%~BT~ ~BT~\|~BT~ ~BT~&~BT~ ~BT~^~BT~ ~BT~~~BT~ ~BT~!~BT~ |

        The longest spelling wins: ~BT~...~BT~ before ~BT~.~BT~, ~BT~!=~BT~ before ~BT~!~BT~. See
        [expressions](expressions.md).

        ## Where the lexer stops reading Hive

        - ~BT~query \{ ... }~BT~ is raw SQL to the matching brace, dedented, with ~BT~\{param}~BT~
          left for the parser. SQL's ~BT~--~BT~ and ~BT~/* */~BT~ comments are recognised; SQL
          keywords keep SQL's upper-case convention. See [queries](queries.md).
        - After ~BT~import~BT~, the path runs to the next whitespace. A quoted path
          (~BT~import "./lib/my file"~BT~) needs no escaping; ~BT~as~BT~ after it is still ~BT~as~BT~.

        *From ~BT~spec/01-lexical.md~BT~ and ~BT~spec/02-grammar.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

func types(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Types

        Every type is known at compile time: no dynamic type, ~BT~Any~BT~ or reflection. A type
        that cannot be worked out is a compile error.

        ## Scalars

        | type | holds |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~Str~BT~ | UTF-8 text |
        | ~BT~Int~BT~ | a 64-bit signed integer — exactly Go's ~BT~int~BT~; past its largest or smallest value it wraps round to the other |
        | ~BT~UInt1~BT~ … ~BT~UInt64~BT~ | an unsigned integer of that many bits: a ~BT~UInt4~BT~ holds 0 to 15 |
        | ~BT~Int1~BT~ … ~BT~Int63~BT~ | a signed integer of that many bits: an ~BT~Int4~BT~ holds −8 to 7 |
        | ~BT~Float~BT~ | a 64-bit float |
        | ~BT~Float32~BT~ | a 32-bit float, computed in single precision |
        | ~BT~Bool~BT~ | ~BT~true~BT~ or ~BT~false~BT~ |
        | ~BT~Atom~BT~ | an interned symbol, written ~BT~#Name~BT~ |
        | ~BT~void~BT~ | nothing — a **return type only**, never a variable, field or element |

        ~BT~Int64~BT~ and ~BT~Float64~BT~ are second names for ~BT~Int~BT~ and ~BT~Float~BT~.

        ### Sized numbers

        - **Wrap at their own width**: a ~BT~UInt4~BT~ at 15 plus one is 0. ~BT~-~BT~ on an unsigned
          type is a compile error.
        - A literal takes the type it lands in, or that of a sized operand beside it
          (~BT~a + 1~BT~ on a ~BT~UInt4~BT~ stays a ~BT~UInt4~BT~), and must fit: ~BT~UInt4 x = 16~BT~ is a
          compile error.
        - **No implicit conversion**: ~BT~Int(b)~BT~ for a ~BT~UInt8~BT~, ~BT~Float(f)~BT~ for a
          ~BT~Float32~BT~; ~BT~UInt4 + UInt8~BT~ is a compile error until one side is converted. Integers of any sizes still compare by value, so ~BT~i < len(v)~BT~
          stays a guard.
        - ~BT~UInt4(x)~BT~ keeps the low bits and never fails; ~BT~UInt4.checked(x)~BT~ answers
          ~BT~Error(x)~BT~ if ~BT~x~BT~ does not fit; ~BT~Float32(x)~BT~ rounds a ~BT~Float~BT~. ~BT~hive.UInt4(x)~BT~
          reaches the builtin past your own declaration.
        - A sized index is its own [bounds](bounds.md) proof: a ~BT~UInt4~BT~ indexes any
          vector of 16 or more unguarded; an unsigned index is never negative.
        - Vectors of them are packed (a million ~BT~UInt4~BT~s take half a megabyte); widths 8,
          16, 32 and 64 are stored as they are.

        ### ~BT~Str~BT~

        Characters, counted by ~BT~len~BT~. It holds bytes (binary data survives a file round
        trip) but is addressed as text.

        - ~BT~s[0]~BT~ is a one-character ~BT~Str~BT~ (there is no character type); ~BT~s[1:3]~BT~ is
          characters one to three, **both bounds inclusive**.
        - Its length is **never** known at compile time: every index is guarded or comes
          from ~BT~indexOf~BT~. ~BT~s[0] = "x"~BT~ is a compile error.
        - Walk the characters with ~BT~split(s, "")~BT~, not by index.

        ### ~BT~Atom~BT~

        ~BT~#Name~BT~: interned to a small integer, declared nowhere, never computed. ~BT~echo~BT~
        prints its name; coerced to ~BT~Str~BT~ it is its decimal value. ~BT~#Nil~BT~ is the only
        built-in atom, always first. **Not a condition**: ~BT~if flag~BT~ is a compile error;
        write ~BT~if flag == #Ready~BT~.

        ## ~BT~Result<T, E>~BT~

        The built-in generic union every fallible operation returns: ~BT~Result.Ok(value)~BT~
        or ~BT~Result.Error(payload)~BT~. Matching both is exhaustive ([patterns](patterns.md)).

        The half of the type a variant does not say comes from where it lands (a
        declaration, a ~BT~return~BT~, a ~BT~Result~BT~ parameter or field, the other side of ~BT~==~BT~)
        or, after ~BT~:=~BT~, from the **first later use** that says it; then it is fixed.
        ~BT~r := Result.Ok(3)~BT~ with no such use is a compile error; write
        ~BT~Result<Int, Str> r = Result.Ok(3)~BT~.

        ## Vectors

        Contiguous and homogeneous. **A name's kind is what its declaration says, never
        what its value showed.**

        | written | means | legal where |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~Str[3]~BT~ | **static**: exactly three | anywhere |
        | ~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~ | **dynamic**: promises nothing about the length | anywhere |
        | ~BT~Str[]~BT~ | **any length** of this element type | **parameters only** |

        - A return or storage is static or ~BT~[dyn]~BT~.
        - **A ~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~ parameter takes only a vector declared ~BT~[dyn]~BT~** (~BT~mut~BT~ or not),
          never a static vector or a literal; a read-only parameter should be ~BT~Str[]~BT~. A
          declaration, field or return declared ~BT~[dyn]~BT~ does take a static vector.
        - **~BT~[dyn]~BT~ is never inferred**: ~BT~:=~BT~ gives a static length.

        ~CODE~hive
        mut v := ["a", "b", "c"]
        append(v, "d")            // compile error: ~BT~v~BT~ is not a dynamic vector

        mut Str[dyn] w = ["a", "b", "c"]
        append(w, "d")            // fine
        ~CODE~

        - ~BT~+~BT~ builds a **new** vector, lengths added; ~BT~==~BT~ compares element by element;
          ~BT~v[lo:hi]~BT~ includes ~BT~hi~BT~. Comparing a vector with a non-vector is a compile
          error. ~BT~Str[3][2]~BT~ is two vectors of three.
        - ~BT~Table~BT~ is an alias for ~BT~Str[dyn][dyn]~BT~ ([tables](tables.md)).

        ## Declared types

        No variants: a struct. Variants: a tagged union.

        ~CODE~hive
        type User \{                 // a struct
        	id:   Int
        	name: Str
        }

        type Shape \{                // a tagged union
        	Circle \{ radius: Int }
        	Rectangle \{ width: Int, height: Int }
        	Point                   // a variant may carry nothing
        }

        type Event \{
        	at: Int                 // outside every variant: added to all of them
        	Opened \{ by: Str }
        	Closed
        }
        ~CODE~

        - Fields may carry ~BT~--~BT~ codec annotations
          ([declarations](declarations.md#field-annotations)).
        - Build by calling the type or variant: ~BT~User(1, "ada")~BT~, ~BT~Shape.Circle(5)~BT~,
          ~BT~Shape.Point()~BT~.
        - **Only ~BT~is~BT~ reaches a variant's fields.** A union-typed value has only the
          fields declared outside its variants (~BT~event.at~BT~), never assigned, since a
          union value is built whole. A field every variant declares itself still
          belongs to the variants.
        - A type may recurse **through a variant**; a struct may not contain itself.

        ## Function types

        ~BT~func(Int): Int~BT~, ~BT~proc(hive.net.HttpRequest): hive.net.HttpResponse~BT~. A ~BT~func~BT~
        value fits a ~BT~proc~BT~ slot, not the reverse. Only a ~BT~proc~BT~ type may have a ~BT~mut~BT~
        parameter (~BT~proc(mut Str[dyn], Str): void~BT~); ~BT~proc(mut Int): void~BT~ and
        ~BT~proc(Int): void~BT~ are different types.

        ## Maps

        ~BT~hive.map.Map<K, T>~BT~ is a module, not a literal ([stdlib/map](stdlib/map.md)).

        - A key is compared and hashed whole: a ~BT~Str~BT~, ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~, ~BT~Bool~BT~, ~BT~Atom~BT~, or
          a declared type whose every field is one of those. **Storage cannot be a key**:
          ~BT~Map<Str[dyn], Int>~BT~ is a compile error.
        - ~BT~hive.map.new()~BT~ takes its type from where it lands or, after ~BT~:=~BT~, from the
          **first later use** (~BT~hive.map.set(m, "a", 1)~BT~); a later use that disagrees is
          a compile error.
        - **By key only**: ~BT~m[0]~BT~ and ~BT~for each~BT~ over a map are compile errors.

        ## Address

        ~BT~Address<M>~BT~ is a [service](stdlib/syslink.md), local or on another machine,
        taking messages of type ~BT~M~BT~. ~BT~spawn~BT~ and ~BT~at~BT~ return one; calling it sends
        (~BT~box(message)~BT~), and the message and the answer are both an ~BT~M~BT~. ~BT~Address~BT~
        without ~BT~M~BT~ is a compile error.

        - ~BT~spawn~BT~ reads ~BT~M~BT~ off its handler. ~BT~at~BT~ takes it from where the address lands
          (~BT~Address<Op> counter = at(#Counter)~BT~) or, after ~BT~:=~BT~, from the **first later
          use** (~BT~counter := at(#Counter)~BT~ then ~BT~counter(Op.Add(1))~BT~); then it is fixed.
        - An address is an ordinary value (stored, passed, carried in a message) with no
          order.
        - ~BT~hive.Address<M>~BT~ reaches the builtin past your own ~BT~Address~BT~.

        ## Secret

        ~BT~Secret<T>~BT~ is a value that must not leak: a password, key, token, or a record
        of them. ~BT~hide(value)~BT~ makes one of any ~BT~T~BT~; only ~BT~reveal(secret)~BT~ opens it.
        Library secrets are ~BT~Secret<Str>~BT~.

        ~CODE~hive
        type Login \{
        	user: Str
        	pin: Int
        }

        if hide(Login("ada", 1234)) is Result.Ok(kept) \{
        	echo reveal(kept).pin
        }
        ~CODE~

        - **~BT~T~BT~ is plain data**: text, numbers, vectors, maps, results and declared types
          of them. A ~BT~Secret~BT~ of a function, address, library handle or ~BT~Secret~BT~ is a
          compile error, as is ~BT~Secret~BT~ without ~BT~T~BT~.
        - **Never shown**: ~BT~echo~BT~, ~BT~panic~BT~ or interpolation of a ~BT~Secret~BT~, or of anything
          holding one, is a compile error; so are ~BT~encode~BT~ and sending one in a message.
        - ~BT~T.decode~BT~ reads a ~BT~Secret<U>~BT~ field as the ~BT~U~BT~ the document holds, kept as
          ~BT~bypass~BT~ would. A value of the wrong kind is reported by kind, never content.
        - Kept in locked memory (never swapped to disk), cleared once nothing holds it.
          Locked memory runs out, so ~BT~hide~BT~ answers ~BT~Result<Secret<T>, SecretError>~BT~:
          ~BT~SecretError.LimitExceeded~BT~ or ~BT~SecretError.Unsupported~BT~, each with a
          ~BT~message~BT~.
        - ~BT~bypass(value)~BT~ is a ~BT~Secret<T>~BT~ in ordinary memory: never shown, swappable,
          cannot fail; for a literal or a ciphertext. ~BT~hive.term.readSecretBypass~BT~ and
          ~BT~hive.crypto.decryptBypass~BT~ are ~BT~readSecret~BT~ and ~BT~decrypt~BT~ into ordinary
          memory, with no limit; ~BT~readSecretBypass~BT~ cannot fail.
        - ~BT~==~BT~ compares contents in constant time; there is no order. A map inside is
          kept in key order: the same pairs are equal, and ~BT~reveal~BT~ returns that order.
        - ~BT~hive.term.readSecret~BT~, ~BT~hive.env.getSecret~BT~ and ~BT~hive.crypto.randomSecret~BT~
          return one as a ~BT~Result~BT~, never passing through a ~BT~Str~BT~.
        - ~BT~hive.Secret<T>~BT~ and ~BT~hive.SecretError~BT~ reach the builtins past your own
          declarations.

        ## Assignability

        A value of type ~BT~S~BT~ fits where ~BT~T~BT~ is expected exactly when:

        1. they are the same type;
        2. ~BT~T~BT~ is ~BT~T'[]~BT~ and ~BT~S~BT~ is ~BT~T'[n]~BT~ or ~BT~T'[dyn]~BT~, element types matching;
        3. ~BT~T~BT~ is ~BT~T'[dyn]~BT~ and ~BT~S~BT~ is ~BT~T'[n]~BT~, except as an argument: a ~BT~T'[dyn]~BT~ parameter takes only a ~BT~T'[dyn]~BT~;
        4. ~BT~T~BT~ is a ~BT~proc~BT~ type and ~BT~S~BT~ is the ~BT~func~BT~ type of the same shape;
        5. ~BT~S~BT~ is ~BT~mut T~BT~ and ~BT~T~BT~ is not ~BT~mut~BT~: the callee gets an immutable copy.

        - **No implicit numeric conversion** of size or kind: write ~BT~Int(b)~BT~,
          ~BT~Float(f)~BT~, ~BT~hive.conv.itf(n)~BT~. ~BT~hive.math.max(0, health)~BT~ is a compile error.
        - **No subtyping, no interfaces.** A variant is not a type: ~BT~Shape.Circle~BT~
          cannot be a parameter's type.
        - **A declared static length is a promise**: a ~BT~Str[3]~BT~ slot takes only a vector
          of exactly three ([bounds](bounds.md)).

        *From ~BT~spec/03-types.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

func declarations(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Declarations

        Imports and declarations come in any order. **No ~BT~pub~BT~/~BT~priv~BT~**: an importer sees
        everything a module declares. Module-level declarations see each other, so
        callables may call each other.

        ## ~BT~type~BT~

        Shapes: [types](types.md#declared-types). Every declared type gets a derived
        deep copy, total order, JSON codec and structural digest, emitted only where
        used, without reflection.

        ### Field annotations

        They set a field's name on the wire and what to read where the wire has nothing.
        ~BT~--~BT~ opens them and each clause ends at its ~BT~;~BT~; a field takes any of them, or
        none, **in any order**: ~BT~-- Default as "light"; JSON as colour_scheme;~BT~ is the
        same as ~BT~theme~BT~ below.

        ~CODE~hive
        type User \{
        	name: Str -- JSON as username;
        	age:  Int
        	firstName: Str -- Default as "";
        	theme: Str -- JSON as colour_scheme; Default as "light";
        }
        ~CODE~

        | written | means |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~JSON as <name>;~BT~ | the key this field is read from and written as |
        | ~BT~URL as <name>;~BT~ | the same, in a [query string](stdlib/net-urls.md) |
        | ~BT~Flag as <name>;~BT~ | the same, as a [command-line flag](stdlib/term.md) without its dashes |
        | ~BT~Flag as <name> <short>;~BT~ | the same, with a short name read after one dash: the shorter of the two |
        | ~BT~Default as <literal>;~BT~ | what to use where the document has ~BT~null~BT~ or nothing at all |

        - Compile-time only: the field is still ~BT~user.name~BT~, ordered, copied and
          compared as before.
        - ~BT~JSON as~BT~, ~BT~URL as~BT~ and ~BT~Flag as~BT~ names ignore Hive's name shapes: ~BT~user_name~BT~
          and ~BT~user-name~BT~ (words joined by touching ~BT~-~BT~) are bare keys; anything else is
          a string, ~BT~"user name"~BT~.
        - **Two fields of one object may not answer to one key** in any format: compile
          error. Each format's names are independent.
        - A flag is named **without its dashes**: ~BT~Flag as dry-run;~BT~ is ~BT~--dry-run~BT~.
          ~BT~Flag as background-task bg;~BT~, names in either order, is ~BT~--background-task~BT~
          and ~BT~-bg~BT~. Two names of one length, a third name, or two fields with one short
          name are compile errors.
        - **~BT~Default as~BT~ takes a literal** the field can hold (~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~, ~BT~Str~BT~,
          ~BT~Bool~BT~ or ~BT~Atom~BT~; a leading ~BT~-~BT~ for a negative number). It covers a key that is
          **absent** or **null**, which is otherwise an error. A **wrong-type** value is
          still an error.
        - ~BT~--~BT~ needs no space after it. ~BT~JSON~BT~, ~BT~URL~BT~, ~BT~Flag~BT~, ~BT~Default~BT~ and ~BT~as~BT~ are not
          reserved words.

        ## ~BT~func~BT~ and ~BT~proc~BT~

        ~CODE~hive
        func greet(name: Str, style: Greeting): Str \{
        	if style is Greeting.Formal(title) \{
        		return "Good evening, \{title} \{name}."
        	}
        	return "Hey \{name}!"
        }

        proc grow(vec: mut Str[dyn], tag: Str): void \{
        	append(vec, tag)
        }
        ~CODE~

        - A ~BT~func~BT~ **may perform I/O** (~BT~echo~BT~, ~BT~hive.file~BT~, ~BT~hive.net~BT~) and **may call
          a ~BT~proc~BT~**. Its one restriction: no ~BT~mut~BT~ parameter (~BT~v: mut T~BT~); a ~BT~mut~BT~ value
          passed to it arrives as an immutable copy. So it cannot write storage its
          caller can see.
        - Parameters are ~BT~name: Type~BT~, or ~BT~name: mut Type~BT~ in a ~BT~proc~BT~. Every callable
          declares a return type, ~BT~void~BT~ included.
        - A program starts at ~BT~proc main(): void~BT~ in the file handed to ~BT~hive build~BT~ /
          ~BT~hive run~BT~; any other ~BT~main~BT~ signature is refused. Arguments come from
          ~BT~hive.term.args()~BT~; the exit status is ~BT~hive.term.exit(code)~BT~.

        ## Returning on every path

        A non-~BT~void~BT~ ~BT~func~BT~ or ~BT~proc~BT~ must return on every path. A path terminates in:

        - ~BT~return~BT~;
        - ~BT~panic~BT~ or a call to ~BT~hive.term.exit~BT~, handy for an unreachable tail (an
          ~BT~assert~BT~ does not count);
        - an ~BT~if~BT~/~BT~else~BT~ whose every branch terminates;
        - a ~BT~switch~BT~ whose every arm terminates ([statements](statements.md));
        - an **else-less** ~BT~if~BT~/~BT~else if~BT~ chain whose every branch terminates and whose
          patterns take every value of its subject: ~BT~Ok~BT~ and ~BT~Error~BT~, every variant of a
          union, nested as deep as they go ([patterns](patterns.md)).

        ~CODE~hive
        func describe(shape: Shape): Str \{
        	if shape is Shape.Circle(r)            \{ return "circle" }
        	else if shape is Shape.Rectangle(w, h) \{ return "rectangle" }
        	else if shape is Shape.Point           \{ return "a point" }
        }                                          // no ~BT~else~BT~ — adding one is an error
        ~CODE~

        ## ~BT~query~BT~

        A ~BT~func~BT~ whose body is inline SQL and whose return type describes its rows:
        [queries](queries.md).

        ## ~BT~test~BT~

        ~CODE~hive
        test "an empty cart costs nothing" \{
        	Item[0] empty = []
        	assert total(empty) == 0
        }
        ~CODE~

        Named in prose; no parameters, no result. ~BT~return value~BT~ inside is a compile
        error; a bare ~BT~return~BT~ leaves early. See [testing](testing.md).

        ## ~BT~law~BT~

        ~CODE~hive
        law cancelledIsFinal(booking: Booking, event: Event) \{
        	if booking.status is Status.Cancelled \{
        		assert apply(booking, event).status == booking.status
        	}
        }
        ~CODE~

        A claim the compiler proves for every value of its parameters; one it cannot
        prove is a compile error. No return type, no ~BT~mut~BT~ parameter, called only by
        another law, never emitted. See [laws](laws.md).

        - Inside a type, with one parameter of it: kept by every value of the type
          ([a type's laws](laws.md#a-types-laws)).
        - Written ~BT~for table <name>~BT~: a SQL filter every query on the table carries
          ([a table's laws](laws.md#a-tables-laws)).

        ## ~BT~import~BT~

        Outside any callable: [modules](modules.md).

        ## How a call runs

        **Every call blocks its caller.** There is no ~BT~async func~BT~, Future or Promise
        type; concurrency is decided where a call is written
        ([concurrency](concurrency.md)).

        *From ~BT~spec/04-declarations.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

func expressions(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Expressions

        ## Precedence, loosest to tightest

        | level | operators | associativity |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | 1 | ~BT~\|\|~BT~ | left |
        | 2 | ~BT~&&~BT~ | left |
        | 3 | ~BT~is~BT~, ~BT~bounds~BT~ | non-associative |
        | 4 | ~BT~>~BT~ ~BT~<~BT~ ~BT~>=~BT~ ~BT~<=~BT~ ~BT~==~BT~ ~BT~!=~BT~ | non-associative |
        | 5 | ~BT~&~BT~ ~BT~\|~BT~ ~BT~^~BT~ ~BT~<<~BT~ ~BT~>>~BT~ | left, one of them at a time |
        | 6 | ~BT~+~BT~ ~BT~-~BT~ | left |
        | 7 | ~BT~*~BT~ ~BT~/~BT~ ~BT~%~BT~ | left |
        | 8 | ~BT~\|>~BT~ | left |
        | 9 | unary ~BT~-~BT~, ~BT~!~BT~, ~BT~~~BT~ | prefix |
        | 10 | ~BT~**~BT~ | right |
        | 11 | a ~BT~with~BT~ clause | postfix |
        | 12 | call, index, slice, member | postfix, left |

        - ~BT~-2 ** 2~BT~ is ~BT~-(2 ** 2)~BT~; ~BT~2 ** -3~BT~ reads the sign as part of the exponent.
        - Bitwise operators bind tighter than a comparison (~BT~x & 1 == 0~BT~ tests ~BT~x & 1~BT~)
          but have **no order** among themselves or against arithmetic: two different
          ones side by side, or one beside ~BT~+ - * / % **~BT~, unparenthesised, is a compile
          error. Write ~BT~(x >> 4) & 0xF~BT~, never ~BT~x >> 4 & 0xF~BT~.
        - ~BT~|>~BT~ takes only the value **beside** it: ~BT~v |> len > 2~BT~ compares the length;
          ~BT~1 + 2 |> f~BT~ is ~BT~1 + f(2)~BT~, so write ~BT~(1 + 2) |> f~BT~.

        ## What the operators do

        | operator | on | yields |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~+~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~ / ~BT~Str~BT~ / vectors | the sum / concatenation / a **new** vector |
        | ~BT~-~BT~ ~BT~*~BT~ ~BT~/~BT~ ~BT~%~BT~ ~BT~**~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~ | arithmetic |
        | unary ~BT~!~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ | the opposite |
        | ~BT~==~BT~ ~BT~!=~BT~ | any two values of **one** type | structural equality |
        | ~BT~<~BT~ ~BT~>~BT~ ~BT~<=~BT~ ~BT~>=~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~, ~BT~Str~BT~ | ordering |
        | ~BT~&&~BT~ ~BT~\|\|~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ | short-circuiting |
        | ~BT~is~BT~ | see [patterns](patterns.md) | a ~BT~Bool~BT~, binding as it matches |
        | ~BT~bounds~BT~ | a vector and an ~BT~Int~BT~ | sugar for ~BT~i >= 0 && i < hive.len(v)~BT~ |
        | ~BT~&~BT~ ~BT~\|~BT~ ~BT~^~BT~ | two integers of one type | bitwise and, or, exclusive or |
        | ~BT~<<~BT~ ~BT~>>~BT~ | an integer, and a count that is unsigned or a literal | shifted; the left side's type |
        | unary ~BT~~~BT~ | an integer | every bit flipped |
        | ~BT~\|>~BT~ | a value and a callable | sugar for the call with the value first |

        Comparing two different types is a compile error, not ~BT~false~BT~. There is **no
        implicit numeric conversion**.

        ## Bits

        - Convert an ~BT~Int~BT~ shift count: ~BT~x << UInt8(n)~BT~. A right shift copies a signed
          type's sign bit.
        - Results stay within the type's width. ~BT~~~BT~ on a literal works in the type it
          lands in: ~BT~UInt8 m = ~0x0F~BT~ is ~BT~0xF0~BT~.
        - A ~BT~Bool~BT~ takes none of these; its operators are ~BT~&&~BT~, ~BT~||~BT~, ~BT~!=~BT~, ~BT~!~BT~.
        - ~BT~<<<a, b, c>>>~BT~ builds a ~BT~UInt8[dyn]~BT~ from its parts in order, most
          significant bit first, each as wide as its type: a ~BT~UInt4~BT~ writes four bits, a
          ~BT~UInt16~BT~ two bytes, a ~BT~UInt8[]~BT~ or ~BT~Str~BT~ its bytes. A literal is a byte unless
          it says otherwise (~BT~0x0D0A: UInt16~BT~); ~BT~little~BT~ writes a part's bytes the other
          way round. The parts must make whole bytes and a run of bytes must start on a
          byte, checked at compile time. Read bytes back with a
          [binary pattern](patterns.md).

        ~CODE~hive
        hash = (hash ^ UInt32(b)) * 0x0100_0193        // ~BT~hash~BT~ a mut UInt32, ~BT~b~BT~ a UInt8
        UInt8[dyn] header = <<<UInt4(4), UInt4(5), 0, length, payload>>>
        ~CODE~

        ## Unary ~BT~!~BT~

        Takes a **~BT~Bool~BT~** only, binding tighter than every comparison, ~BT~&&~BT~ and ~BT~||~BT~.
        ~BT~found == false~BT~ is an ordinary alternative.

        ~CODE~hive
        echo !isEven(3)                // the call is the operand, not ~BT~isEven~BT~
        if !found && ready \{ }         // (!found) && ready
        if !(a == b) \{ }               // parentheses, because ~BT~==~BT~ is looser
        if !(v bounds i) \{ }           // the same, and this proves nothing about ~BT~i~BT~
        ~CODE~

        **~BT~!~BT~ over an expression holding an ~BT~is~BT~ is a compile error**, reaching through
        ~BT~&&~BT~, ~BT~||~BT~ and a second ~BT~!~BT~ and no further: a negated match cannot narrow or
        bind. Put the other case in the ~BT~else~BT~.

        ~CODE~hive
        if !(x is Shape.Circle(r)) \{ }        // compile error
        if !(ready && (x is Shape.Point)) \{ } // the same, through the ~BT~&&~BT~
        if !a && (x is P) \{ }                 // fine: negates only ~BT~a~BT~
        ~CODE~

        ## Interpolation

        ~BT~"loaded \{len(rows)} of \{total}"~BT~ renders each value as ~BT~echo~BT~ does; ~BT~\\{~BT~ is a
        literal brace. It is **the only implicit conversion**, and only to text.

        ## Calls and named arguments

        Funcs, procs, queries and type constructors (library record types included) take
        arguments by name: ~BT~f(b: 1, "s")~BT~.

        - Named arguments go anywhere; unnamed ones keep their order.
        - Names must exist and not repeat. **Once any argument is named, the call must
          cover the full parameter list.**
        - Arguments are evaluated in the order written.
        - Builtins and library calls take the names their pages give:
          ~BT~join(sep: ",", vector: v)~BT~, ~BT~hive.math.clamp(value: x, low: 0.0, high: 1.0)~BT~.

        ### Piping

        ~BT~x |> f(a)~BT~ is ~BT~f(x, a)~BT~; left-associative, so a chain reads in run order:

        ~CODE~hive
        echo nums |> filter(isEven) |> map(label) |> join(" ")
        ~CODE~

        The right side is a call, a callable's name or a constructor; ~BT~x |> f~BT~ and
        ~BT~x |> f()~BT~ are both ~BT~f(x)~BT~. Procs, builtins and constructors all pipe; a ~BT~_~BT~ in
        a stage makes that call a partial application.

        ## Function values

        ~CODE~hive
        f := takes                              // a bare reference
        handler(_, db)                          // a partial application
        hive.net.httpServe(8080, handler(_, db))
        ~CODE~

        - Each ~BT~_~BT~ becomes a parameter, **in hole order**. Supplied values are captured
          by value where written, not re-read at each call.
        - A **constructor** partially applies (~BT~Msg.Changed(_)~BT~ is a ~BT~func(Str): Msg~BT~)
          but has **no bare reference**: ~BT~Msg.Changed~BT~ is a compile error.
        - A callable with a **~BT~mut~BT~ parameter** is a value (~BT~grow(_, "x")~BT~ is a
          ~BT~proc(mut Str[dyn]): void~BT~); a ~BT~mut~BT~ position stays a hole.
        - A call through a function value is checked against its type (argument count
          and types, a ~BT~mut~BT~ variable wherever it says ~BT~mut~BT~) and is positional only.
        - **Never values**: a builtin or library call (wrap it in your own ~BT~func~BT~), and a
          generic callable.
        - A statically-sized parameter's promise is part of the function type
          ([bounds](bounds.md)).

        ## Member access, indexing, slicing

        ~BT~x.field~BT~ reads a field; ~BT~x.member~BT~ reads through an import alias or a library
        module's name. ~BT~v[i]~BT~ indexes; ~BT~v[lo:hi]~BT~ slices with **both bounds inclusive**
        (~BT~t[1:3]~BT~ is three elements), either bound omissible (~BT~v[:2]~BT~, ~BT~v[1:]~BT~, ~BT~v[:]~BT~).
        All are proved in range at compile time ([bounds](bounds.md)).

        ## Arithmetic at the edges

        | expression | result |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~a / 0~BT~, ~BT~a % 0~BT~ | ~BT~0~BT~ — division by zero is a value, not a crash |
        | ~BT~Int~BT~ overflow | wraps round: ~BT~MAX + 1~BT~ is ~BT~MIN~BT~, ~BT~MIN - 1~BT~ is ~BT~MAX~BT~ |
        | ~BT~2 ** 100~BT~ | ~BT~0~BT~ — that wrap, reached by repeated multiplication |
        | ~BT~-MIN~BT~, ~BT~MIN * -1~BT~, ~BT~MIN / -1~BT~ | ~BT~MIN~BT~ |
        | ~BT~n ** k~BT~, ~BT~k < 0~BT~ on ~BT~Int~BT~ | ~BT~0~BT~, including ~BT~1 ** -1~BT~ |
        | ~BT~n ** 0~BT~ | ~BT~1~BT~ |
        | ~BT~-7 % 3~BT~ | ~BT~-1~BT~ — the remainder takes the sign of the dividend |
        | ~BT~10.0 ** 400.0~BT~ | ~BT~+Inf~BT~ — ~BT~Float~BT~ arithmetic does produce non-finite values |

        Converting a non-finite or too-large ~BT~Float~BT~ to an ~BT~Int~BT~ is the **one**
        unspecified case; check the range first.

        ## ~BT~with~BT~ clauses

        One clause per ~BT~with~BT~:

        - **~BT~with timeout <ms>~BT~** bounds a wait; the result becomes
          ~BT~Result<T, hive.task.TimeoutError>~BT~ ([concurrency](concurrency.md)).
        - **~BT~with compiler~BT~** runs the call while the program builds and builds its
          answer in ([build-time](build-time.md)).

        Decoding is not a ~BT~with~BT~ clause: ~BT~User.decode(text, hive.json.codec())~BT~.

        ## Evaluation order

        Left to right; ~BT~&&~BT~ / ~BT~||~BT~ short-circuit, so ~BT~if x is T.A(v) && v == "ok"~BT~ is
        legal. Every expression is evaluated **exactly once** each time control reaches
        it, an ~BT~assert~BT~'s operand included.

        *From ~BT~spec/05-expressions.md~BT~ and ~BT~spec/02-grammar.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

func statements(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Statements

        ## Bindings

        ~CODE~hive
        x := expr                    // inferred
        Str[dyn] v = expr            // annotated
        mut x := expr                // inferred, reassignable
        mut Str[dyn] v = expr        // annotated, reassignable
        _ := expr                    // throws the value away
        ~CODE~

        - ~BT~:=~BT~ infers the type, **static vector length included**. A typed ~BT~=~BT~ is the
          only way to say ~BT~[dyn]~BT~, to make ~BT~Str[3]~BT~ a promise rather than an
          observation, or to give a ~BT~hive.map.new()~BT~ somewhere to land.
        - Only ~BT~[dyn]~BT~ grows; a ~BT~mut~BT~ vector with an inferred length is rebound only to
          one of the same length.
        - A call that answers with nothing cannot be bound.
        - **Immutable unless ~BT~mut~BT~**: no reassigning, writing through (~BT~v[i] = …~BT~,
          ~BT~v.f = …~BT~) or ~BT~append~BT~. The spelling never decides it; ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~ is an
          optional spelling for an immutable constant ([syntax](syntax.md)).

        ## Binding with a pattern

        ~CODE~hive
        Result.Ok(text) := hive.file.read(path) else \{
        	Result.Error(file.FileError.NotFound(_, _)) \{ return Result.Ok(defaults()) }
        	Result.Error(why)                           \{ return Result.Error(why.message) }
        }
        echo text
        ~CODE~

        - The pattern binds immutably for the **rest of the block**; the ~BT~else~BT~ holds
          ~BT~switch~BT~ arms, one position each, for every value it does not take.
        - **Every arm must leave**: ~BT~return~BT~, ~BT~panic~BT~, ~BT~break~BT~ or ~BT~continue~BT~.
        - ~BT~switch~BT~ rules hold: one evaluation, every value taken, no arm that can never
          run.

        ## Assignment

        ~CODE~hive
        x = expr
        v[i] = expr
        rec.field = expr
        b.items[0] = expr
        ~CODE~

        The target is a ~BT~mut~BT~ binding or a path into one; a parameter or plain ~BT~:=~BT~
        binding is a compile error. ~BT~+= -= *= /=~BT~, ~BT~&= |= ^= <<= >>=~BT~, ~BT~++~BT~ and ~BT~--~BT~
        mean ~BT~x = x <op> …~BT~.

        ## ~BT~if~BT~

        ~BT~if cond \{ } else if cond \{ } else \{ }~BT~. ~BT~cond~BT~ is a ~BT~Bool~BT~: **no truthiness**;
        an ~BT~Atom~BT~, ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Str~BT~ or vector is a compile error. Names bound by an ~BT~is~BT~ in
        the condition are in scope in that branch's body and in the rest of the
        condition after ~BT~&&~BT~.

        ## ~BT~switch~BT~

        ~CODE~hive
        switch shape, fill \{
        	Shape.Circle(r), Fill.Solid(color) \{ }
        	Shape.Point, _                     \{ }
        	_, _                               \{ }
        }
        ~CODE~

        - Each subject is evaluated **exactly once**, left to right, before any arm.
        - One position per subject: a pattern (as after ~BT~is~BT~), a literal the value must
          equal (~BT~0~BT~, ~BT~-1~BT~, ~BT~2.5~BT~, ~BT~#Ready~BT~, ~BT~true~BT~), or ~BT~_~BT~.
        - The **first** arm whose every position matches runs; no fall-through.
          ~BT~break~BT~ / ~BT~continue~BT~ act on the enclosing loop.
        - An arm's bindings are scoped to its body; it binds a name once across its
          positions.
        - **Always exhaustive, and every arm must be able to run**, or it is a compile
          error ([patterns](patterns.md)). If every arm returns, the path terminates.

        ## Loops

        ~CODE~hive
        for i := 0; i < 10; i++ \{ }
        for ; cond; \{ }                      // any clause may be omitted: a while loop

        for each name in values \{ }
        for each name: T in values \{ }       // the annotation states the element type
        ~CODE~

        - The counter is loop-scoped and **implicitly mutable** (no ~BT~mut~BT~, never
          ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~). Neither the init nor the post clause may be ~BT~async~BT~.
        - ~BT~for each~BT~ binds each vector element to an **immutable** name; it never
          indexes, so it needs no bounds proof. Over a map it is a compile error.
        - ~BT~break~BT~ leaves the innermost loop; ~BT~continue~BT~ skips to its next iteration.
          Either outside a loop is a compile error.

        ## ~BT~echo~BT~, ~BT~assert~BT~, ~BT~panic~BT~

        - ~BT~echo value~BT~ writes one line to stdout, from a ~BT~func~BT~ or a ~BT~proc~BT~: an atom's
          **name**, a vector's elements, a map's pairs in its own order, a declared
          value's fields. A ~BT~Str~BT~ on its own is unquoted; inside any of those it is
          quoted, so ~BT~["1", "2"]~BT~, ~BT~[1, 2]~BT~ and ~BT~["1 2"]~BT~ differ.
        - ~BT~assert cond~BT~: outside a ~BT~test~BT~, a failure stops the program and prints the
          condition as written and both sides of an ~BT~==~BT~ or ~BT~!=~BT~; inside a ~BT~test~BT~, it is
          recorded and the suite carries on. Operands are evaluated exactly once.
        - ~BT~panic value~BT~ stops the program immediately, rendering ~BT~value~BT~ as ~BT~echo~BT~
          does. It takes any value and never returns, so it terminates a path. In a
          [service](stdlib/syslink.md) it kills only that service.

        ## ~BT~async~BT~ as a statement

        ~BT~async f(x)~BT~ runs the call on its own virtual thread and carries on
        ([concurrency](concurrency.md)).

        ## Expression statements

        Only a call stands alone, its result discarded: ~BT~x + 1~BT~ is refused;
        ~BT~_ := x + 1~BT~ drops a value on purpose.

        - A discarded **~BT~sort~BT~** on a ~BT~mut~BT~ vector sorts **in place**; one that cannot is
          a compile error.
        - A discarded **~BT~drop~BT~** is the ordinary way to remove elements.

        ## Scope

        A name is visible from its declaration to the end of its block and declared
        **once per block** (~BT~_~BT~ as often as you like). An inner block may shadow it; a
        local binding shadows a module-level declaration or a builtin. **No forward
        reference inside a body.**

        *From ~BT~spec/06-statements.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

func patterns(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Patterns

        ~BT~x is P~BT~ is a ~BT~Bool~BT~, written in an ~BT~if~BT~ or as a ~BT~switch~BT~'s arms
        ([statements](statements.md)). A match narrows ~BT~x~BT~ and binds parts of it to new names.

        ## Variant patterns

        ~CODE~hive
        value is Type.Variant(a, b)
        value is Type.Variant          // a variant that carries nothing

        if shape is Shape.Circle(r) \{
        	echo r
        } else if shape is Shape.Rectangle(w, _) \{
        	echo w
        }
        if indexOf(names, "bob") is Result.Ok(i) \{ }     // the call runs once
        ~CODE~

        * Fields bind **by position**; ~BT~_~BT~ skips one; a literal or pattern in a field's
          place must match (see below).
        * **Name every field or none.**
        * An imported type goes through its module's name: ~BT~style is text.Align.Left~BT~.

        ## Vector patterns

        ~CODE~hive
        v is ["a", x]                  // exactly two; first equals "a", second binds
        v is ["a", x, ...rest]         // at least two; ~BT~rest~BT~ binds the leftovers
        ~CODE~

        A position is a literal (~BT~"a"~BT~, ~BT~3~BT~, ~BT~#Atom~BT~, ~BT~true~BT~), a name, ~BT~_~BT~, or a pattern.
        **Without ~BT~...rest~BT~ the vector must have exactly that many elements**; with it, at least.

        ## String patterns

        ~CODE~hive
        path is "/health"                        // no holes: an exact match
        path is "/users/\{id}/posts/\{postId}"
        path is "/files/\{rest}"                  // a trailing hole runs to the end
        if line is "\{user is (\w+)}@\{host is ([\w.]+)}" \{    // a hole may take only what its regex matches
        	echo "\{user} at \{host}"
        }
        ~CODE~

        * The template must match the **whole** string. Holes are **non-greedy**: one
          between two ~BT~/~BT~ never takes a ~BT~/~BT~.
        * Compile errors: a hole that is not a plain binding name; two **open** holes side by side.
        * **Nothing inside a hole's parentheses is escaped or interpolated**:
          ~BT~\{year is (\d\{4})}~BT~ is four digits. Holes go in ~BT~"..."~BT~ patterns, never backtick ones.
        * The regex is Go's RE2, read at compile time. Compile errors: a malformed regex,
          a backreference, a lookaround, a named group. A group inside a hole does not capture.

        ## Binary patterns

        ~BT~<<<...>>>~BT~ reads bits in order, most significant first, each field taking its
        type's width:

        ~CODE~hive
        if packet is <<<version: UInt4, ihl: UInt4, _: UInt8, length: UInt16, ...rest>>> \{
        	echo "IPv\{version}, \{length} bytes"
        }
        if file is <<<0x89, "PNG", size: UInt32, body: UInt8[size], ...more>>> \{ ... }
        ~CODE~

        | field | reads |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~name: UInt4~BT~ | that many bits, bound as that type |
        | ~BT~name~BT~ | one byte |
        | ~BT~0x45~BT~, ~BT~0x45: UInt16~BT~, ~BT~"PNG"~BT~ | a literal to match |
        | ~BT~_: UInt3~BT~ | bits skipped |
        | ~BT~body: UInt8[size]~BT~ | a run of bytes; its length is an earlier field or anything in scope |
        | ~BT~...rest~BT~ | what is left: a ~BT~Str~BT~ for a ~BT~Str~BT~ subject, a ~BT~UInt8[dyn]~BT~ otherwise |

        * Multi-byte fields are big-endian; ~BT~size: UInt32 little~BT~ reads little-endian.
        * The subject is a ~BT~UInt8[]~BT~, a ~BT~Str~BT~ (its bytes, not copied) or an integer whose
          bits must all be taken: ~BT~op is <<<code: UInt4, reg: UInt4>>>~BT~ on a ~BT~UInt8~BT~.
        * The match checks the length; no field needs a bounds proof.
        * Compile errors: fields that come to part of a byte; a run, string or rest starting mid-byte.
        * A ~BT~switch~BT~ over binary patterns ends with ~BT~_~BT~.

        ## Nested patterns

        A binding position (a variant's field, a vector's element) may hold a pattern:

        ~CODE~hive
        if foo() is Result.Ok(Foo.Bar(["foo/\{padding}/bar", ...rest])) \{ }

        // compiled exactly as:
        if foo() is Result.Ok(result) && result is Foo.Bar(values) && values is [head, ...rest] && head is "foo/\{padding}/bar" \{ }
        ~CODE~

        A chain's rules hold: outer parts first, the subject evaluated once, bindings
        visible in the rest of the condition and the body, no ~BT~!~BT~.

        * A variant's position may hold a **literal**: ~BT~Result.Ok(3)~BT~, ~BT~Pair.Of("bees", n)~BT~.
        * **A pattern binds a name once**: ~BT~Pair.Of(a, Result.Ok(a))~BT~ is a compile error.
        * **A variant pattern matches only its own type**, at any depth.

        ## Exhaustiveness

        An **else-less** ~BT~if~BT~/~BT~else if~BT~ chain covering every value of its subject is a
        terminating path, so it needs no dead ~BT~else~BT~:

        ~CODE~hive
        func describe(shape: Shape): Str \{
        	if shape is Shape.Circle(r)            \{ return "circle" }
        	else if shape is Shape.Rectangle(w, h) \{ return "rectangle" }
        	else if shape is Shape.Point           \{ return "a point" }
        }
        ~CODE~

        * **Coverage follows nesting**: ~BT~Result.Ok(Foo.Bar(_))~BT~, ~BT~Result.Ok(Foo.Baz)~BT~ and
          ~BT~Result.Error(_)~BT~ cover ~BT~Result<Foo, E>~BT~; so does the equivalent hand-written chain.
        * A vector is covered by length (~BT~[]~BT~, ~BT~[x, ...rest]~BT~), a ~BT~Bool~BT~ by ~BT~true~BT~,
          ~BT~false~BT~. Numbers, atoms and strings have no finite cover; only ~BT~"\{s}"~BT~ covers
          every string.
        * The subject is a variable or a path of fields and indexes from one, never a
          call. A chain may cover any one subject its branches open by matching.
        * A branch whose condition goes on past its pattern (~BT~r is Result.Ok(n) && n > 0~BT~) covers nothing.
        * A short chain's error names a value no branch takes: ~BT~r~BT~ when it is ~BT~Result.Ok(Foo.Baz)~BT~.

        ## ~BT~switch~BT~

        Two rules beyond a chain's, both compile errors:

        * **Every value has an arm**, coverage counted as above over all subjects
          together: ~BT~Shape.Point, _~BT~ covers a point with any fill. The error names an
          uncovered value (~BT~Shape.Circle(_), Fill.None~BT~). A number, atom or string
          subject needs ~BT~_~BT~. A call may be a subject; it is evaluated once.
        * **Every arm can run**: an arm the arms above fully take (anything after
          ~BT~_, _~BT~, a literal written twice) is refused by number, ~BT~arm 3~BT~.

        A binding's ~BT~else~BT~ (~BT~Result.Ok(n) := r else \{ ... }~BT~) follows both, the pattern
        being its first arm. ~BT~Fuzz.Variable()~BT~ matches only a variant with no fields;
        ~BT~Fuzz.Variable~BT~ or ~BT~Fuzz.Variable(_)~BT~ matches it whatever it carries.

        ## Narrowing and scope

        * An ~BT~is~BT~ binding is **immutable**, in scope in the rest of the condition after
          ~BT~&&~BT~ and in the branch body, **not in the ~BT~else~BT~**.
        * Outside its branch, a union value has only the fields every variant shares.
        * **A match cannot be negated**: ~BT~!(x is Shape.Circle(r))~BT~ is a compile error;
          use the ~BT~else~BT~. See [expressions](expressions.md).

        *From ~BT~spec/07-patterns.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

func mutability(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Mutability and value semantics

        ## ~BT~mut~BT~

        Variables are immutable by default. ~BT~mut~BT~ permits reassignment, writing through
        the name (~BT~v[0] = …~BT~, ~BT~rec.f = …~BT~) and ~BT~append~BT~.

        * ~BT~mut~BT~ is compile-time only: ~BT~mut T~BT~ is ~BT~T~BT~ at run time.
        * A ~BT~T~BT~ parameter or return accepts a ~BT~mut T~BT~ (the callee gets an immutable
          copy), never the reverse.
        * Anywhere else a type is written, ~BT~mut~BT~ is refused: **on a field, on a return,
          in a ~BT~func(...)~BT~ type**. A ~BT~proc(...)~BT~ type may mark a parameter ~BT~mut~BT~.
        * A ~BT~mut~BT~ variable may not be ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~.

        ## ~BT~mut~BT~ parameters

        **Only a ~BT~proc~BT~** may take one, and it receives the caller's storage:

        ~CODE~hive
        proc grow(vec: mut Str[dyn], tag: Str): void \{
        	append(vec, tag)
        }

        mut Str[dyn] v = ["a"]
        grow(v, "b")          // waited for: v is now ["a", "b"]
        async grow(v, "c")    // fired off: the thread grows a copy; v is untouched
        ~CODE~

        * The argument must be a ~BT~mut~BT~ variable or a path into one.
        * **The call site decides**: a call waited for there shares the caller's storage
          (reassignment, element writes, ~BT~append~BT~); an ~BT~async~BT~ or ~BT~await~BT~ed call gets a
          copy. Likewise through a function value: after ~BT~f := grow~BT~, ~BT~f(v, "d")~BT~ grows
          ~BT~v~BT~, ~BT~async f(v, "e")~BT~ a copy.
        * In a partial application a ~BT~mut~BT~ position stays a hole: ~BT~grow(_, "f")~BT~.
        * Passing a variable to a ~BT~mut~BT~ parameter, directly or through a function
          value, loses every length and index fact [already proved](bounds.md) about it.
        * **One call may not lend a vector and a path inside it**: ~BT~both(v, v[0])~BT~ is a
          compile error. ~BT~swap(v[i], v[j])~BT~ and ~BT~put(box, box.count)~BT~ are fine.
        * A packed vector's element is lent as a copy and written back after the call.

        ## Value types

        Vectors, ~BT~Table~BT~s, maps and declared types containing them are **value types**:
        two names never see each other's mutations unless both are ~BT~mut~BT~, which share
        storage. **Storage an immutable binding observes is never mutated in place afterwards.**

        A binding whose right side **names existing storage** (~BT~ys := xs~BT~,
        ~BT~ys := xs[i]~BT~, ~BT~ys := rec.field~BT~) may copy; a **fresh** one (a literal, a ~BT~+~BT~
        concatenation, a function result) never does.

        | target ⟵ source | decision |
        | --- | --- |
        | immutable ⟵ immutable | **alias** — neither side can ever mutate it |
        | ~BT~mut~BT~ ⟵ ~BT~mut~BT~ | **share** — shared mutable state is the intent |
        | ~BT~mut~BT~ ⟵ immutable | **alias** if the target is never written through, else **copy** |
        | immutable ⟵ ~BT~mut~BT~ | **alias** if the source is never mutated again, else **copy** |

        An **assignment** is classified the same way, its target always ~BT~mut~BT~:
        ~BT~v = xs~BT~, ~BT~v[i] = xs~BT~ and ~BT~rec.f = xs~BT~ copy what an immutable ~BT~xs~BT~ names,
        unless it lands in a field a copy of the record would share.

        An alias is chosen only when provably indistinguishable from a copy. An
        in-place ~BT~sort~BT~ counts as a write:

        ~CODE~hive
        mut Int[dyn] a = [3, 1, 2]
        b := a          // copies, because...
        sort(a)         // ...this rewrites ~BT~a~BT~, and ~BT~b~BT~ must not follow
        ~CODE~

        ### Two ~BT~mut~BT~ bindings share storage, not names

        ~CODE~hive
        mut Str[dyn] a = ["x", "y", "z"]
        mut Str[dyn] b = a
        append(b, "w")            // len(a) is now 4
        a[0] = "changed"          // b[0] is "changed" too
        b = ["replaced"]          // len(a) is still 4: ~BT~b~BT~ alone was rebound
        ~CODE~

        **Rebinding is not a write through**: ~BT~b~BT~ gets its own storage and the sharing
        ends. Not shared at all: a source naming different storage on each read
        (~BT~mut b = a[i]~BT~), and a name standing for more than one binding in a body.

        ## What a copy copies

        Deep, by static type, without reflection: a flat vector's backing array; every
        level of a nested vector or ~BT~Table~BT~; a map's key order and lookup; a declared
        type's storage-owning fields; nothing for a scalar-only type. A ~BT~mut T~BT~
        parameter, the one place storage crosses a call on purpose, is a pointer.

        *From ~BT~spec/08-mutability-and-values.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

func concurrency(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Concurrency

        **Every call blocks its caller**; the call site decides how. There is no
        ~BT~async func~BT~, Future or Promise type. ~BT~f(x)~BT~ waits and yields ~BT~T~BT~, as a plain
        call (no goroutine, no channel).

        ## ~BT~async f(x)~BT~

        As a statement: runs the call on its own virtual thread and carries on; the
        result is discarded. Works on any call (~BT~func~BT~, ~BT~proc~BT~, ~BT~query~BT~, library,
        service) except three, refused by name: a **global builtin**, a
        **constructor**, a **partial application**.

        ## ~BT~x := async f(a)~BT~

        ~CODE~hive
        rows  := async loadRows()              // Str[dyn] — starts now
        total := async countAll()              // Int      — starts now, alongside
        echo "loaded \{len(rows)} of \{total}"   // waits for both, here
        ~CODE~

        * ~BT~x~BT~ has the call's own return type (~BT~Str~BT~, not a Future); there is no handle type.
        * **Reading ~BT~x~BT~ waits** if the call has not finished; later reads are free and
          answer the same value.
        * A panic in the call is raised at the **first read**; if ~BT~x~BT~ is never read, the
          work still runs and the panic is dropped.
        * Refused: the three calls above, a ~BT~void~BT~ call, ~BT~mut~BT~, ~BT~with timeout~BT~.

        ## ~BT~await [...]~BT~

        The **only** ~BT~await~BT~. Each call starts on its own thread; the list is one
        barrier yielding, **in order**, a statically sized vector of results (~BT~Str[3]~BT~),
        never ~BT~[dyn]~BT~ or ~BT~Any~BT~. Three one-second calls take about a second.

        * A list of one is legal.
        * Over ~BT~void~BT~ calls it is a **statement**.
        * Every entry is a **call**, all of the **same type**; name differing calls with
          ~BT~async~BT~ instead. ~BT~await []~BT~ is a compile error.

        ## ~BT~with timeout~BT~

        Bounds any wait written where it happens:

        ~CODE~hive
        f(x) with timeout 500
        await [f(a), f(b)] with timeout 500

        if slowShout("worth waiting for") with timeout 100 is Result.Error(err) \{
        	echo "gave up after \{err.waited}ms"
        }
        ~CODE~

        * The wait yields ~BT~Result<T, hive.task.TimeoutError>~BT~, the error carrying
          ~BT~waited~BT~ and ~BT~message~BT~.
        * On an await-all: **one deadline for the whole barrier**.
        * It abandons the **waiting, not the work**: the call runs on, its result dropped.
        * Refused on a ~BT~void~BT~ call.
        * On a request to a [service](stdlib/syslink.md) it folds into the request's own
          error rather than wrapping a second ~BT~Result~BT~.

        ## Threads and ~BT~mut~BT~ arguments

        A ~BT~mut~BT~ argument shares the caller's storage **only when the caller waits for
        the call there**. A call on its own thread (~BT~async f(v)~BT~, ~BT~n := async f(v)~BT~,
        each call in ~BT~await [...]~BT~) gets a copy of **every** argument, evaluated by the
        caller before the thread starts, so later changes do not reach it and a call
        nested in an argument runs in the caller. Literals are exempt. See
        [mutability](mutability.md).

        There are no channels, locks, thread handles, cancellation tokens or scheduler
        settings. For long-lived shared state use a [service](stdlib/syslink.md) (one
        message at a time, no lock needed).

        *From ~BT~spec/09-concurrency.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

func buildTime(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Build time

        **~BT~f(x) with compiler~BT~ runs the call during the build**; the executable holds
        its answer in place of the call and only reads it at run time.

        ~CODE~hive
        proc main(): void \{
        	// countries.csv, read once by the build: the program carries the map, not the file.
        	names := countries() with compiler
        	if hive.map.get(names, "BR") is Result.Ok(name) \{
        		echo name
        	}
        }
        ~CODE~

        ## Rules

        * **Only in the entrypoint** (the file given to ~BT~hive build~BT~); the function may
          come from any module.
        * **After a call**, binding as tightly as ~BT~with timeout~BT~: ~BT~x |> f() with compiler~BT~
          is ~BT~f(x) with compiler~BT~. Usable wherever a value is; never with ~BT~async~BT~.
        * **The function is named**, not held in a variable.
        * **Arguments** must be known before the program runs: literals (~BT~3~BT~, ~BT~-1.5~BT~,
          ~BT~"a"~BT~ with nothing interpolated, ~BT~true~BT~, ~BT~#Ready~BT~); vectors and constructors
          of them (~BT~[1, 2]~BT~, ~BT~Point(1, 2)~BT~, ~BT~Result.Ok(1)~BT~, ~BT~UInt8(3)~BT~); another
          ~BT~with compiler~BT~ call's answer, inline or through a name the body binds
          **once**, without ~BT~mut~BT~ (a name bound twice anywhere, e.g. shadowed in an
          inner block, is not accepted):

        ~CODE~hive
        listing := releases() with compiler
        readmes := readmesOf(listing) with compiler    // handed what the first answered
        ~CODE~

        * **The answer is data**: numbers, text, ~BT~Bool~BT~, atoms, vectors, maps,
          ~BT~Result~BT~s, declared types made of them, library error unions included. A
          function, ~BT~Address~BT~, ~BT~Secret~BT~, codec, query, library-owned type
          (~BT~hive.json.JsonValue~BT~, ~BT~hive.net.HttpResponse~BT~) or no answer is a compile error.
        * Each read is the program's own copy; changing a ~BT~mut~BT~ binding of it does not
          affect the next read.

        ## When it runs

        * **~BT~hive build~BT~, ~BT~run~BT~, ~BT~test~BT~ and ~BT~export~BT~** run every call on every build,
          before compiling the program: on this machine whatever the target, **from the
          entrypoint's folder** (relative paths read as under ~BT~hive run~BT~). Nothing is
          kept between builds.
        * **~BT~hive check~BT~, ~BT~hive emit~BT~ and the language server never run one**; its type
          is the function's declared answer.
        * A call runs after every call it waits on: one in its arguments, and one inside
          any function its run may reach. Two waiting on each other are a compile error.
        * **A panic or failed ~BT~assert~BT~ is a compile error** at the call, giving what it
          panicked with. Any other ending (~BT~hive.term.exit~BT~, a crash on another thread)
          is reported with what it printed.
        * Its output appears under its line: ~BT~main.hive:12: read 7 countries~BT~.

        *From ~BT~spec/05-expressions.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

func proofs(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Bounds

        Every index and slice must be **proved in range at compile time**; anything
        unproved is a **compile error**. Generated code never fails out of bounds.

        ## Proofs

        * **Known length, literal index**: ~BT~v[2]~BT~ on a ~BT~Str[3]~BT~ compiles; ~BT~v[3]~BT~ does not.
        * **A guard**: ~BT~if i >= 0 && i < len(v) \{ v[i] }~BT~, or its short form ~BT~if v bounds i \{ v[i] }~BT~.
        * **A counting ~BT~for~BT~** (~BT~for i := 0; i < len(v); i++~BT~) whose counter starts at a
          non-negative literal, steps by one and is held strictly below something. ~BT~Int~BT~
          wraps past ~BT~MAX~BT~, so a larger step, or no upper limit, proves nothing.
        * **An index from ~BT~indexOf~BT~.**
        * **A sized integer** whose top the known length is past: a ~BT~UInt4~BT~ indexes any
          vector of 16 or more; an ~BT~Int4~BT~ one of 8 or more, under an ~BT~i >= 0~BT~ guard.
          Against a ~BT~[dyn]~BT~ vector an unsigned index needs only ~BT~i < len(v)~BT~. ~BT~Int(i)~BT~
          is an ~BT~Int~BT~ and proves nothing.
        * **A literal mask** the known length is past: ~BT~buckets[hash & 255]~BT~ on a ~BT~Str[256]~BT~.
        * **~BT~for each~BT~**, which does not index.

        Bind any other computed index to a variable and guard it:

        ~CODE~hive
        echo v[len(v) - 1]                  // compile error: a computed index

        last := len(v) - 1
        if v bounds last \{ echo v[last] }   // fine
        ~CODE~

        ## Lengths through operations

        | operation | length of the result |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~a + b~BT~ | the two added — ~BT~Str[2] + Str[8]~BT~ fills a ~BT~Str[10]~BT~ |
        | ~BT~map(v, f)~BT~ | ~BT~v~BT~'s, unchanged |
        | ~BT~sort(v)~BT~ / ~BT~sort(v, first)~BT~ | ~BT~v~BT~'s, unchanged |
        | ~BT~filter~BT~, ~BT~filterMap~BT~ | **unknown, always** |
        | ~BT~split~BT~, ~BT~hive.map.keys~BT~, most library calls | unknown |

        These compose: ~BT~map(a, f) + sort(b)~BT~ is as long as ~BT~a + b~BT~. If **either** side
        of a concatenation is unknown, so is the result; there is no "at least".

        ## Declared and inferred lengths

        A declared length is a promise: **every** value entering a ~BT~Str[3]~BT~ slot
        (initialiser, assignment, argument, constructed field, return) must have
        exactly three elements. So ~BT~v[2]~BT~ stays legal after reassignment, and a
        ~BT~Str[3]~BT~ parameter or field is indexed unguarded.

        ~CODE~hive
        mut Str[3] v = ["a", "b", "c"]
        v = ["x", "y", "z"]              // fine — still three
        v = ["x"]                        // compile error: ~BT~v~BT~ is declared ~BT~Str[3]~BT~
        Str[3] parts = split(line, ",")  // compile error: that length isn't known
        ~CODE~

        **A callable value carries the promise.** ~BT~f := takes~BT~ is a ~BT~proc(Str[3]): void~BT~;
        calls through it, or a parameter, field or vector holding it, are checked like
        direct ones. Passing ~BT~takes~BT~ to a ~BT~proc(Str[]): void~BT~ parameter is a compile error.

        An inferred length survives writes *through* the name and is lost when the name
        is rebound, even in a branch that may not run:

        ~CODE~hive
        mut v := ["a", "b", "c"]
        v[0] = "x"                  // still three
        if changed \{ v = ["x"] }
        echo v[2]                   // compile error: v's length is no longer known
        ~CODE~

        ## ~BT~indexOf~BT~

        ~CODE~hive
        found := indexOf(names, "bob")
        if found is Result.Ok(i) \{
        	echo "\{i}: \{names[i]}"   // no guard needed
        }
        ~CODE~

        The proof holds for the searched vector while it means the same thing: an index
        found in ~BT~a~BT~ still needs a guard to index ~BT~b~BT~.

        ## What loses a proof

        * **rebinding** the vector, even in a branch that may not run;
        * **~BT~drop~BT~**, the one builtin that shortens a vector;
        * passing it to a **~BT~mut~BT~ parameter**, directly or through a function value;
        * replacing a **field** that held it (what was proved about the old value is lost).

        **Writing an element (~BT~v[i] = x~BT~) keeps everything proved about ~BT~v~BT~**, in
        branches and loops too, but loses what was proved *through* its elements: after
        ~BT~grid[0] = [1]~BT~, a guard on ~BT~grid[0]~BT~ must be written again.

        ~BT~drop(v, low, high)~BT~ removes ~BT~low~BT~ through ~BT~high~BT~ **inclusive**, proved like a
        slice; crossed bounds remove nothing.

        ## ~BT~Str~BT~

        Indexed, sliced and proved like a vector, but **a ~BT~Str~BT~'s length is never known
        to the compiler**:

        ~CODE~hive
        s := "abc"
        echo s[1]                    // compile error, even though ~BT~s~BT~ is right there
        if s bounds 1 \{ echo s[1] }  // fine

        if indexOf(line, "=") is Result.Ok(at) \{
        	echo line[at:]           // no guard: the search proved it
        }
        ~CODE~

        A slice's two bounds are proved separately: a guard on one says nothing about the other.

        *From ~BT~spec/10-bounds.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

func generics(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Generics

        A name in a signature that is neither a builtin nor a declared type is a **type
        variable**, making the callable generic in it.

        ~CODE~hive
        func first(v: T[]): Result<T, Bool> \{
        	if len(v) > 0 \{
        		return Result.Ok(v[0])
        	}
        	return Result.Error(false)
        }
        ~CODE~

        Variables are inferred from the **parameters**, including inside a parameter's
        own type; **type arguments are never written at the call site**. Here ~BT~T~BT~ comes
        from the vector passed, ~BT~K~BT~ and ~BT~E~BT~ from the function:

        ~CODE~hive
        func filterMap(values: T[], transform: func(T): Result<K, E>): K[dyn] \{
        	mut K[dyn] out = []
        	for each value in values \{
        		if transform(value) is Result.Ok(next) \{
        			append(out, next)
        		}
        	}
        	return out
        }
        ~CODE~

        The body may name the variables: ~BT~mut K[dyn] out = []~BT~, ~BT~for each v: T in …~BT~, ~BT~Box<T> b = …~BT~.

        ## Generic types

        A type whose fields mention variables is generic in them, in **first-appearance
        order**. Type arguments go on the **value's declaration**, which also tells a
        variant constructor which instantiation it builds:

        ~CODE~hive
        type Box \{
        	items: T[dyn]
        	label: Str
        }

        type Either \{
        	Left  \{ left: A }
        	Right \{ right: B }
        }

        Box<Str> people = Box(["ada", "grace"], "people")
        Either<Str, Int> answer = Either.Right(42)
        ~CODE~

        Each instantiation has its own clone, JSON codec and structural digest:
        ~BT~Either<Str, Int>~BT~ and ~BT~Either<Int, Str>~BT~ are different types down to the wire.

        ## Monomorphization

        Calls are resolved at compile time from the argument types; **one copy is
        emitted per distinct set of type arguments** (~BT~first_Str~BT~, ~BT~first_Int~BT~), with no
        boxing, dispatch or reflection. Each copy is checked separately: an
        instantiation at ~BT~Str[3]~BT~ is held to that length; one at ~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~ guards its indexes.

        ## Refused

        * **A generic callable as a value**: ~BT~f := first~BT~ is a compile error.
        * **A variable only in the return type**: ~BT~func makeOne(n: Int): T~BT~ is a
          compile error at the call.
        * **Runaway expansion** is capped; overrunning it is a compile error.

        *From ~BT~spec/11-generics.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

func modules(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Modules

        ~BT~import~BT~, outside any callable, brings another file's declarations into scope.

        ~CODE~hive
        import ./lib/text
        import ./lib/inventory as stock

        proc main(): void \{
        	echo text.repeat("=", 28)
        	echo stock.line(stock.Item("Beeswax", 450))
        }
        ~CODE~

        | written | names |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~import ./lib/text~BT~ | a Hive file on this disk — ~BT~.hive~BT~ is **never** written |
        | ~BT~import ./lib/util.go~BT~ | a Go file on this disk — ~BT~.go~BT~ **always** is |
        | ~BT~import https://host/owner/repo/src/foo~BT~ | a file in a git repository |
        | ~BT~import hive.ui~BT~ | a standard library module, which names no file |

        * A path with a space may be **quoted**, nothing escaped inside: ~BT~import "./lib/my file"~BT~.
        * A path is **relative to the importing file's directory**, not where the compiler ran.
        * A module is reached by its file's name (~BT~./lib/text~BT~ → ~BT~text~BT~) or its ~BT~as~BT~
          name, which is ~BT~camelCase~BT~ and not ~BT~hive~BT~.
        * **Everything a module declares is visible**; there is no ~BT~pub~BT~/~BT~priv~BT~.
        * An imported type is constructed, annotated and matched through its module's
          name: ~BT~text.Align.Left()~BT~, a ~BT~text.Align~BT~ parameter, ~BT~style is text.Align.Left~BT~.
          Two files may each declare an ~BT~Align~BT~; they stay distinct types.
        * A file is **loaded once** however many modules import it. **Import cycles**
          of any length **are rejected**, with the loop printed.
        * Errors name the file and line they are on, an imported module's included.

        ## Importing a Go file

        ~CODE~hive
        import ./lib/measures.go

        proc main(): void \{
        	echo measures.grams(measures.Weight(899, "Hive tool"))
        }
        ~CODE~

        * **Every value is deep-copied both ways**, so an imported Go function is a
          ~BT~func~BT~, not a ~BT~proc~BT~.
        * **Only Go exports are reachable**: a callable with its first letter lowered
          (~BT~Grams~BT~ is ~BT~measures.grams~BT~), a type in its PascalCase.
        * Signatures are read by the Go toolchain.

        | Go | Hive |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~string~BT~ / ~BT~int~BT~ / ~BT~float64~BT~ / ~BT~bool~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ / ~BT~Int~BT~ / ~BT~Float~BT~ / ~BT~Bool~BT~ |
        | ~BT~[]T~BT~ | ~BT~T[dyn]~BT~; a parameter takes ~BT~T[]~BT~ |
        | ~BT~[][]string~BT~ | ~BT~Table~BT~ |
        | ~BT~map[K]V~BT~ | ~BT~hive.map.Map<K, T>~BT~, with a scalar key |
        | a struct the file exports | a Hive type of the same name and shape |
        | ~BT~(T, error)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<T, Str>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~error~BT~ alone | ~BT~Result<Bool, Str>~BT~, whose ~BT~Ok~BT~ carries ~BT~true~BT~ |
        | no result | a function answering with nothing |

        * ~BT~Int~BT~ is Go's ~BT~int~BT~; ~BT~int64~BT~ and the rest are **rejected**, not converted.
        * Refused with a reason: a pointer, channel, interface, function value,
          variadic parameter, fixed-size array, type from another package, unexported
          field. A struct crosses only if *every* field does.
        * A Go map arrives with its keys **sorted**.

        ## Importing from a git repository

        ~CODE~hive
        import https://github.com/owner/repo/src/text
        import https://github.com/owner/repo@a1b2c3/src/text        // pinned
        import https://github.com/owner/repo/go/util.go as helpers
        ~CODE~

        * ~BT~https://host/owner/repo~BT~ is the repository, the rest the path inside it. A
          revision follows the repository: ~BT~repo@a1b2c3~BT~, ~BT~repo@v1.2.0~BT~, ~BT~repo@main~BT~.
        * The module is **named after the file inside the repository**.
        * Fetched **once**, into a cache shared by every program on the machine.
        * No revision: **pinned on first use** in a lock file beside the entrypoint
          (~BT~main.hive~BT~ → ~BT~main.hive-lock~BT~). Commit it; delete a line to take the latest again.
        * Needs ~BT~git~BT~ on the PATH.

        ## The standard library

        **Every module answers to its short name with no import**: ~BT~task.sleep(10)~BT~,
        ~BT~conv.sti(text)~BT~ and a ~BT~ui.View~BT~ parameter are ~BT~hive.task.sleep(10)~BT~,
        ~BT~hive.conv.sti(text)~BT~ and ~BT~hive.ui.View~BT~. As with a [builtin](builtins.md), a
        declaration, import, local or parameter of the same name wins, per module;
        ~BT~hive.<module>~BT~ always reaches the library.

        ~CODE~hive
        import ./lib/clock as time

        proc main(): void \{
        	echo time.now()         // lib/clock's
        	echo hive.time.now()    // the library's
        	task.sleep(10)          // the library's
        }
        ~CODE~

        * ~BT~import hive.net as web~BT~ renames a module: ~BT~web.httpServe~BT~ is ~BT~hive.net.httpServe~BT~.
        * Import a module only: ~BT~import hive.ui~BT~, never ~BT~import hive.ui.View~BT~. ~BT~import hive~BT~ is invalid.
        * An unknown module (~BT~import hive.jsno~BT~) is refused at the import.
        * **A ~BT~hive.*~BT~ module is linked only when the program references it**; an import alone links nothing.

        *From ~BT~spec/12-modules.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

func builtins(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Builtins

        Always in scope. Several are overloaded by argument type, so a builtin is only
        called: never used as a value or partially applied. Its arguments may be named,
        as in ~BT~join(sep: ",", vector: v)~BT~; ~BT~|~BT~ joins the names of an overloaded position:
        ~BT~len(vector|str|map)~BT~, ~BT~bytes(vector|str)~BT~, ~BT~append~BT~/~BT~prepend(vector, value)~BT~,
        ~BT~drop(vector, low, high)~BT~, ~BT~join(vector, sep)~BT~, ~BT~split(str, sep)~BT~, ~BT~trim(str)~BT~,
        ~BT~replaceFirst~BT~/~BT~replaceAll(str, from, to)~BT~, ~BT~indexOf(vector|str, value|sub)~BT~,
        ~BT~row~BT~/~BT~column(table, key)~BT~, ~BT~toTable(cells, wide)~BT~, ~BT~map~BT~/~BT~filterMap(values, transform)~BT~,
        ~BT~filter(values, keep)~BT~, ~BT~sort(values, first)~BT~, ~BT~encode(value, codec)~BT~,
        ~BT~spawn(handler, state, name)~BT~, ~BT~at(endpoint, name)~BT~, ~BT~kill(address)~BT~,
        ~BT~hide~BT~/~BT~bypass(value)~BT~, ~BT~reveal(secret)~BT~, and ~BT~value~BT~ for a number type's conversion.

        | function | signature | what it does |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~len~BT~ | ~BT~(T[]): Int~BT~ / ~BT~(Str): Int~BT~ / ~BT~(Map<K,T>): Int~BT~ | elements / **characters** / pairs |
        | ~BT~bytes~BT~ | ~BT~(T[]): Int~BT~ / ~BT~(Str): Int~BT~ | byte footprint / **bytes** of the UTF-8 |
        | ~BT~append~BT~ | ~BT~(mut T[dyn], T): void~BT~ | grows a mutable dynamic vector in place |
        | ~BT~prepend~BT~ | ~BT~(mut T[dyn], T): void~BT~ | the same, at the **front** |
        | ~BT~drop~BT~ | ~BT~(mut T[dyn], Int, Int): T[dyn]~BT~ | removes ~BT~low~BT~–~BT~high~BT~ **inclusive**, hands them back |
        | ~BT~join~BT~ | ~BT~(Str[], Str): Str~BT~ | concatenates, ~BT~sep~BT~ between elements |
        | ~BT~split~BT~ | ~BT~(Str, Str): Str[dyn]~BT~ | splits on ~BT~sep~BT~ |
        | ~BT~replaceFirst~BT~ | ~BT~(Str, Str, Str): Str~BT~ | the first ~BT~from~BT~, rewritten |
        | ~BT~replaceAll~BT~ | ~BT~(Str, Str, Str): Str~BT~ | every ~BT~from~BT~, rewritten |
        | ~BT~trim~BT~ | ~BT~(Str): Str~BT~ | without the white space at either end |
        | ~BT~indexOf~BT~ | ~BT~(T[], T): Result<Int, Bool>~BT~ / ~BT~(Str, Str): …~BT~ | position of the first match |
        | ~BT~row~BT~ | ~BT~(Table, Str): Str[dyn]~BT~ | the row whose first cell equals ~BT~key~BT~, else ~BT~[]~BT~ |
        | ~BT~column~BT~ | ~BT~(Table, Str): Str[dyn]~BT~ | the column whose top cell equals ~BT~key~BT~, else ~BT~[]~BT~ |
        | ~BT~toTable~BT~ | ~BT~(Str[], Int): Table~BT~ | the cells cut into rows that wide, the last one short |
        | ~BT~map~BT~ | ~BT~(T[], func(T): K): K[dyn]~BT~ | every element, transformed |
        | ~BT~filter~BT~ | ~BT~(T[], func(T): Bool): T[dyn]~BT~ | the elements ~BT~keep~BT~ says yes to |
        | ~BT~filterMap~BT~ | ~BT~(T[], func(T): Result<K, E>): K[dyn]~BT~ | transform and select in one pass |
        | ~BT~sort~BT~ | ~BT~(T[]): T[dyn]~BT~ / ~BT~(T[], func(T, T): Bool): T[dyn]~BT~ | in order |
        | ~BT~encode~BT~ | ~BT~(T, hive.codec.Codec<E>): Str~BT~ | written in the format the codec names |
        | ~BT~spawn~BT~ | ~BT~(handler, S): Result<Address<M>, SyslinkError>~BT~ / ~BT~(handler, S, Atom): …~BT~ | starts a service, optionally registered under a name |
        | ~BT~at~BT~ | ~BT~(Atom): Address<M>~BT~ / ~BT~(Str, Atom): Address<M>~BT~ | a named service on this node / on the node at an endpoint, ~BT~M~BT~ read off where it lands |
        | ~BT~kill~BT~ | ~BT~(Address<M>): void~BT~ | stops a service |
        | ~BT~hide~BT~ | ~BT~(T): Result<Secret<T>, SecretError>~BT~ | a value as a [~BT~Secret~BT~](types.md#secret), never swapped to disk |
        | ~BT~bypass~BT~ | ~BT~(T): Secret<T>~BT~ | a value as a ~BT~Secret~BT~ in ordinary memory; cannot fail |
        | ~BT~reveal~BT~ | ~BT~(Secret<T>): T~BT~ | what a ~BT~Secret~BT~ holds |
        | ~BT~UInt4~BT~, every number type | ~BT~(integer): UInt4~BT~ | the value kept to the type's bits; never fails |
        | ~BT~UInt4.checked~BT~ | ~BT~(T): Result<UInt4, T>~BT~ | the value, or ~BT~Error(value)~BT~ where it does not fit |

        * ~BT~len~BT~ and ~BT~bytes~BT~ differ only for strings: ~BT~"café"~BT~ has ~BT~len~BT~ 4, ~BT~bytes~BT~ 5.
        * ~BT~replaceFirst~BT~ and ~BT~replaceAll~BT~ **answer a new string**, match plain text (not
          a pattern), and are not an error when ~BT~from~BT~ is absent.
        * ~BT~trim~BT~ strips every Unicode white space (spaces, tabs, line ends, no-break
          spaces) from both ends only: ~BT~trim(" a b\n")~BT~ is ~BT~"a b"~BT~.
        * ~BT~toTable~BT~ fills rows left to right, the last taking what is left:
          ~BT~toTable(["1", "2", "3", "4", "5", "6", "7"], 3)~BT~ is
          ~BT~[["1", "2", "3"], ["4", "5", "6"], ["7"]]~BT~. A width below one is a compile
          error when **literal**; when **computed**, it answers an empty ~BT~Table~BT~.
        * ~BT~encode~BT~ has no runtime function: the encoder is written from the argument's
          static type at the call site (total, no reflection). The codec is an
          **ordinary value**; its type, ~BT~hive.codec.Codec<E>~BT~, says the format. The
          reverse is ~BT~T.decode(text, codec)~BT~; see [json](stdlib/json.md).
        * ~BT~spawn~BT~, ~BT~at~BT~ and ~BT~kill~BT~ are [services](stdlib/syslink.md). A handler is
          ~BT~proc(mut S, M): M~BT~, or ~BT~proc(mut S, M, Address<M>): M~BT~ to be handed its own
          address every turn; ~BT~spawn~BT~ takes either. A second ~BT~spawn~BT~ under a name still
          held fails as ~BT~SyslinkError.Taken~BT~; one with no name is always ~BT~Ok~BT~. ~BT~at~BT~
          looks nothing up; its ~BT~M~BT~ comes from where it lands or its first later use.

        ## Your own declaration wins

        A ~BT~func~BT~, ~BT~proc~BT~, ~BT~query~BT~ or type named like a builtin is what bare calls mean;
        the builtin stays reachable as ~BT~hive.<name>~BT~:

        ~CODE~hive
        func len(label: Str, extra: Int): Str \{ return "\{label}\{extra}" }

        proc main(): void \{
        	Str[dyn] v = ["a", "b"]
        	echo len("x", 2)          // ours: "x2"
        	echo hive.len(v)          // the builtin: 2
        	echo hive.join(v, "-")    // the long name works either way
        }
        ~CODE~

        * A local binding or parameter shadows one the same way; shadowing is **per module**.
        * Only the builtin ~BT~append~BT~ requires a ~BT~mut~BT~ target; only the builtin ~BT~indexOf~BT~
          answers an index the bounds pass accepts unguarded.
        * Library module names (~BT~conv~BT~, ~BT~task~BT~, ~BT~ui~BT~ and the rest) are held the same
          way, and also yield to an import of that name ([modules](modules.md)).

        ## ~BT~map~BT~, ~BT~filter~BT~, ~BT~filterMap~BT~

        * The function is a ~BT~func~BT~ or ~BT~proc~BT~, **never one taking its element ~BT~mut~BT~**.
        * ~BT~filter~BT~ wants a ~BT~Bool~BT~ answer, ~BT~filterMap~BT~ a ~BT~Result~BT~, ~BT~map~BT~ *something*: a
          ~BT~void~BT~ function collects nothing.
        * **Sequential**: once per element, in order, on the calling thread. To run calls
          together, use the [await-all](concurrency.md).
        * ~BT~map~BT~ is **length-preserving** and the [bounds pass](bounds.md) knows it;
          ~BT~filter~BT~ and ~BT~filterMap~BT~ are not.

        ## ~BT~append~BT~, ~BT~prepend~BT~, ~BT~drop~BT~

        Each needs a **~BT~mut T[dyn]~BT~**. ~BT~prepend~BT~ answers nothing, so it is only a
        statement. ~BT~drop~BT~ (both ends inclusive) is the one builtin that **shortens** a
        vector, so it costs that vector whatever the bounds pass had proved.

        ## ~BT~sort~BT~

        Answers a new vector, the original untouched. **One argument** uses the element
        type's order:

        | element | order |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~ | numeric, ascending |
        | ~BT~Str~BT~ | by UTF-8 byte order |
        | ~BT~Bool~BT~ | ~BT~false~BT~ before ~BT~true~BT~ |
        | ~BT~Atom~BT~ | by its compiled value |
        | a vector, a ~BT~Table~BT~ | lexicographic; on a shared prefix the shorter first |
        | ~BT~Result<T, E>~BT~ | every ~BT~Error~BT~ before every ~BT~Ok~BT~ |
        | a struct | field by field, in declaration order |
        | a tagged union | by **variant** first, in declaration order, then its fields |
        | a map, a function value, a service address, or a type holding one | none — a compile error |

        **Two arguments** order by your ~BT~func~BT~ answering whether its first element comes
        before its second; this also sorts a type with no default order. The sort is
        **stable**; both forms preserve length.

        A ~BT~sort~BT~ **statement** on ~BT~mut~BT~ storage sorts it in place:

        ~CODE~hive
        mut Str[dyn] names = ["pear", "apple"]

        sort(names)                  // in place — ~BT~names~BT~ is now sorted
        sorted := sort(names)        // NOT in place — a new vector, ~BT~names~BT~ untouched

        Int[dyn] v = [3, 1, 2]
        sort(v)                      // compile error: ~BT~v~BT~ is not ~BT~mut~BT~
        sort(split(line, ","))       // compile error: nothing to sort in place
        ~CODE~

        A discarded ~BT~sort~BT~ that cannot sort in place is a compile error. Any ~BT~mut~BT~
        storage works: ~BT~sort(b.items)~BT~, ~BT~sort(rows[i])~BT~.

        *From ~BT~spec/13-builtins.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

func tables(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Tables

        ~BT~Table~BT~ is an alias for ~BT~Str[dyn][dyn]~BT~, rows of cells. ~BT~hive.file.csv~BT~ yields
        one, ~BT~hive.sql.unsafe~BT~ answers one, ~BT~hive.map.toTable~BT~ builds one. Builtins:

        ~CODE~hive
        row(table, key)      // the row whose first cell equals ~BT~key~BT~, else []
        column(table, key)   // the column whose top cell equals ~BT~key~BT~, else []

        toTable(cells, 3)    // rows three cells wide, the last one short
        ~CODE~

        ## Readers

        Each format has its own reader; the unused ones stay out of the build.

        | call | yields |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~hive.file.csv(path, separator)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Table, hive.file.TableError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~hive.file.xlsx(path)~BT~ / ~BT~hive.file.ods(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Table[dyn], hive.file.TableError>~BT~: every sheet of a workbook / table of an ODS |
        | ~BT~hive.sql.run(connection, query)~BT~ | whatever the query declared its rows to be |
        | ~BT~hive.sql.unsafe(connection, text)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Table, hive.sql.SqlError>~BT~, for SQL built at run time |

        * **The separator is always written**, ~BT~","~BT~ or ~BT~"\t"~BT~; Hive has no optional parameters.
        * xlsx and ods give one ~BT~Table~BT~ per sheet, in document order; an empty sheet is
          an empty ~BT~Table~BT~. Both readers are dependency-free.
        * ~BT~hive.file.TableError~BT~ has a ~BT~path~BT~ and a ~BT~message~BT~. Unlike
          ~BT~hive.file.FileError~BT~, it means the file opened but did not hold what it claimed.

        ~CODE~hive
        if hive.file.csv("./colonies.csv", ",") is Result.Ok(sheet) \{
        	for each line in sheet \{
        		echo join(line, " | ")
        	}
        }
        ~CODE~

        ## Spreadsheet cells

        Cells arrive as stored (numbers, booleans and cached formula results untouched),
        except **xlsx dates**, stored as day counts: the cell's number format identifies
        them and they are rendered as ~BT~2026-07-25~BT~ (or with a time, or time-only). ODS
        stores real dates. **Rows are padded to their sheet's widest row.**

        *From ~BT~spec/14-stdlib.md~BT~ §14.6.*
    `)
}

func queries(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Queries

        A ~BT~query~BT~ is a ~BT~func~BT~ whose body is **inline SQL** and whose **return type
        describes its rows**. Calling it runs nothing: it answers a
        ~BT~hive.sql.Fragment<rows>~BT~ (the text plus its bound values), an ordinary value
        that ~BT~hive.sql.run(<connection>, <query>)~BT~ runs.

        ~CODE~hive
        type User \{
        	id:   Int
        	name: Str
        }

        query findUser(name: Str): User[dyn] \{
        	SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE name = \{name}
        }

        query userNames(): Str[dyn] \{      // one column needs no row type
        	SELECT name FROM users
        }

        query deleteUser(id: Int): void \{  // a statement reports what it touched
        	DELETE FROM users WHERE id = \{id}
        }
        ~CODE~

        | declared | ~BT~q(...)~BT~ is | ~BT~hive.sql.run(conn, q(...))~BT~ yields |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~Row[dyn]~BT~ (a declared type) | ~BT~Fragment<Row[dyn]>~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Row[dyn], hive.sql.SqlError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~, ~BT~Int[dyn]~BT~, … | ~BT~Fragment<Str[dyn]>~BT~ | that column, as a vector |
        | ~BT~Table~BT~ | ~BT~Fragment<Table>~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Table, hive.sql.SqlError>~BT~ — a header row, then every row as text |
        | ~BT~void~BT~ | ~BT~Fragment<void>~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Int, hive.sql.SqlError>~BT~ — the rows it affected |

        ## Rules

        * ~BT~hive.sql.run~BT~ given anything but a fragment is a compile error naming what it got.
        * **Columns match fields by name**, not ~BT~SELECT~BT~ order. Alias a column named
          unlike its field: ~BT~SELECT u.name AS author~BT~. A row type holds scalars only.
        * **~BT~SELECT *~BT~ fills only a ~BT~Table~BT~**; against a declared row type it is a
          compile error. The rule is about the *result*: ~BT~count(*)~BT~, ~BT~a * 2~BT~, a star in
          a subquery and a ~BT~void~BT~ statement's select list still compile.
        * **Values are bound, never spliced**: ~BT~\{param}~BT~ becomes a placeholder, the value
          sent beside the text. Queries are written with ~BT~?~BT~, rewritten to ~BT~$1, $2, …~BT~
          for PostgreSQL by the connection.
        * The body is SQL: ~BT~--~BT~ and ~BT~/* */~BT~ comments are recognised, SQL keywords keep
          SQL's upper case, and a name colliding with one is quoted: ~BT~SELECT "order" FROM …~BT~.

        ## Optional filters: ~BT~WHERE \{ }~BT~

        ~CODE~hive
        query findColonies(apiary: Str, minFrames: Int, small: Bool, huge: Bool): Colony[dyn] \{
        	SELECT name, apiary, frames FROM colonies
        	WHERE \{
        		if apiary != ""  \{ apiary = \{apiary} }
        		if minFrames > 0 \{ frames >= \{minFrames} }
        		or \{
        			if small \{ frames < 4 }
        			if huge  \{ frames > 10 }
        		}
        	}
        	ORDER BY name
        }
        ~CODE~

        * The block ANDs the predicates whose conditions hold; a nested ~BT~or \{ }~BT~ or
          ~BT~and \{ }~BT~ flips the connective.
        * A group contributing nothing disappears; with no predicate there is **no
          ~BT~WHERE~BT~ clause**. A group contributing more than one is parenthesised.
        * Each branch's text is fixed at compile time; only which branches are taken is
          decided at run time.
        * **A column name or sort direction is never a parameter** (~BT~ORDER BY \{col}~BT~
          sorts by a constant string): make the choices a variant type and dispatch to
          one query per ordering.
        * **SQL you assemble yourself** goes through ~BT~hive.sql.unsafe~BT~: untyped, and
          outside every [table law](laws.md#a-tables-laws).

        Connections come from [~BT~hive.sql~BT~](stdlib/sql.md).

        *From ~BT~spec/04-declarations.md~BT~ §4.5.*
    `)
}

func testing(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Testing

        A ~BT~test~BT~ is a top-level declaration with no parameters and no result. ~BT~assert~BT~ is the only assertion; there is no framework and nothing to register.

        ~CODE~hive
        import ./cart

        test "an empty cart costs nothing" \{
        	cart.Item[0] empty = []
        	assert cart.total(empty) == 0
        }
        ~CODE~

        | command | runs |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~hive test <entrypoint.hive>~BT~ | every test in every file the entrypoint reaches |
        | ~BT~hive test <directory>~BT~ (~BT~hive test .~BT~) | tests of every ~BT~.hive~BT~ file under it that git does not ignore, and of every file outside it one of those imports; each file's once |

        Tests may sit beside the code or in their own file; a file of only tests needs no ~BT~main~BT~. **A run exits non-zero when any test fails.**

        ## ~BT~assert~BT~ in a test

        - A failed ~BT~assert~BT~ fails the test and the suite runs on (outside a test it stops the program). A test that **panics** fails alone too.
        - **Operands are evaluated exactly once**, pass or fail: ~BT~assert parseSum(p) == 42~BT~ runs ~BT~parseSum~BT~ once.
        - A failed comparison shows both sides:

        ~CODE~
          PASS  an empty cart costs nothing
          FAIL  lines add up
                cart.test.hive:33: assert cart.total(two) == 17
                  left:  16
                  right: 17

          2 tests: 1 passed, 1 failed
        ~CODE~

        ## Coverage

        Every run reports it:

        ~CODE~
          6 tests: 6 passed
          coverage: 87.5% of statements (7/8)
          never exercised: describe
        ~CODE~

        It counts statements of your own declarations, not the clone helpers, ordering helpers, atom table or JSON marshalling the compiler emits. ~BT~never exercised~BT~ names declarations no test reached. Several files add a per-file breakdown.

        ## Working directory

        A test runs in the **generated project's** directory, not the entrypoint's folder (which ~BT~hive run~BT~ uses), so **a relative path does not resolve next to the source**. A test needing files writes them into its own directory with ~BT~hive.file.makeDir~BT~ and ~BT~hive.file.write~BT~.

        ## What runs it

        ~BT~go test~BT~, on a generated ~BT~main_test.go~BT~ in ~BT~main.go~BT~'s Go package, so a test reaches every proc, func and type without exporting them.

        *From ~BT~spec/16-testing.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

func laws(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Laws

        A ~BT~law~BT~ is a claim the compiler proves for every value of its parameters: each ~BT~assert~BT~ its body reaches must always hold. **An unprovable law is a compile error**, with no override. Laws never run, except [a type's](#a-types-laws), which decoders check on what they read; [a table's](#a-tables-laws) is a filter every query's SQL must keep.

        ~CODE~hive
        law cancelledIsFinal(booking: Booking, event: Event) \{
        	if booking.status is Status.Cancelled \{
        		assert apply(booking, event).status == booking.status
        	}
        }
        ~CODE~

        ## Writing one

        - ~BT~camelCase~BT~ name; **no return type, no ~BT~mut~BT~ parameter** (both errors).
        - The body may hold anything a ~BT~func~BT~ body may. **Each ~BT~assert~BT~ it reaches is the claim**, where its enclosing ~BT~if~BT~s and patterns hold, and a fact for later statements.
        - **Nothing it reaches may panic or fail an ~BT~assert~BT~**; a law without ~BT~assert~BT~ claims just that: ~BT~law parses(text: Str) \{ _ := nightsOf(text) }~BT~.

        ## Calling a law

        Only a law may call a law, as a statement; elsewhere, or as a value, is an error. A call is a hint, never run: **another law** lends its claim for those arguments; **itself** is induction and must pass, in a parameter's place, **a part a pattern took from that parameter** (a vector's rest, an element, a variant's field). **No circle of laws calling each other.**

        ~CODE~hive
        law replayKeepsTheRoom(booking: Booking, log: Event[dyn]) \{
        	if log is [first, ...rest] \{
        		applyKeepsTheRoom(booking, first)                  // another law
        		replayKeepsTheRoom(apply(booking, first), rest)    // itself, on less
        	}
        	assert replay(booking, log).room == booking.room
        }
        ~CODE~

        Hints first, claim last. Most laws need none: the compiler tries induction on each vector parameter.

        ## What a law may reach

        Only code that **comes to an end**; anything else is an error naming the path to it. This limits only what a law reaches; a ~BT~func~BT~ may still do I/O.

        | reached | |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~func~BT~ and ~BT~proc~BT~ bodies, local ~BT~mut~BT~ | yes |
        | recursion on a part a pattern took from a parameter | yes |
        | ~BT~for each~BT~, a counting loop of the shape [bounds](bounds.md) trusts | yes |
        | any other loop, two callables calling each other | no |
        | a ~BT~hive.*~BT~ call, a builtin, a ~BT~query~BT~ | yes — [below](#library-calls) |
        | ~BT~async~BT~, the await-all, ~BT~with timeout~BT~ | yes — [below](#library-calls) |
        | a function from an imported Go file | no |
        | ~BT~echo~BT~ | yes, ignored |

        ## Library calls

        **Nothing a law reaches is run.** A library call is:

        - **Settled** (same answer for the same arguments on every machine): ~BT~hive.conv~BT~, ~BT~hive.math~BT~, ~BT~hive.map~BT~, hashing and base64 in ~BT~hive.crypto~BT~, ~BT~hive.net.urlEncode~BT~, ~BT~hive.term.positional~BT~, the ~BT~hive.ui~BT~ views, the builtins, a ~BT~query~BT~ (its text and arguments). **Worked out** on known arguments (~BT~hive.conv.sti("42")~BT~ is ~BT~Result.Ok(42)~BT~); otherwise **one unknown per set of arguments**, so a guard and a law asking the same agree.
        - **The world's**: reading or changing the clock, a file, the network, the terminal, the environment, a random source, a service or the time zone (~BT~hive.time.format~BT~ too). **Each call may answer anything its type allows, afresh**: ~BT~hive.time.now() == hive.time.now()~BT~ is not proved. Writes, prints, ~BT~exec~BT~ and ~BT~exit~BT~ are allowed, never performed.

        Also:

        - **A function handed to the library may not fail**: it is called on values the prover never sees.
        - **Servers may panic** when they cannot listen on their port, as may ~BT~hive.syslink.setKey~BT~ on an empty key; a law reaching one is not proved.
        - **~BT~async f(x)~BT~ and ~BT~x := async f(x)~BT~ are the call itself**, on copies; ~BT~await [...]~BT~ is the vector of its calls; **~BT~with timeout~BT~** is the value and a ~BT~TimeoutError~BT~ that may say anything.
        - **A sine and its kind are never worked out**, only equal for equal arguments (a fused multiply-add may round otherwise).
        - **A ~BT~hive.map.Map~BT~ is followed write by write**: ~BT~get~BT~ and ~BT~has~BT~ answer what the key's last ~BT~set~BT~, ~BT~delete~BT~, ~BT~plus~BT~ or ~BT~minus~BT~ left; a key that may or may not match splits the case; a map parameter answers alike per key; ~BT~keys~BT~, ~BT~values~BT~ and ~BT~len~BT~ are worked out for a map from ~BT~new()~BT~ with every key known. Counterexamples try the empty map and one-pair maps.

        ## What values mean

        - **~BT~Int~BT~ wraps** as at run time: ~BT~a + 1 > a~BT~ fails at ~BT~a = 9223372036854775807~BT~. Division by zero is ~BT~0~BT~.
        - **No vector or string holds over 2^60 elements**, so ~BT~len(v) + 1~BT~ does not wrap.
        - **A ~BT~Float~BT~ is carried and compared**; nothing about float arithmetic is proved.

        ## When a law is refused

        ~CODE~
        main.hive:12: in law ~BT~grows~BT~: it does not hold for a = 9223372036854775807: ~BT~a + 1 > a~BT~ is false
        main.hive:30: in law ~BT~countNeverNegative~BT~: cannot prove ~BT~0 <= 1 + count(the rest of v)~BT~
            in the case v is [the first of v, ...the rest of v]
            known: 0 <= count(the rest of v)
        main.hive:41: in law ~BT~steady~BT~: cannot prove ~BT~hive.time.now() == hive.time.now()~BT~
        ~CODE~

        A counterexample, an open goal with its case and known facts, or what could not be followed. → **help an open goal in the law's body** (split the case, or call a law settling what is missing), or narrow the law with an ~BT~if~BT~.

        ## A type's laws

        A law **inside a type**, with one parameter of that type, holds for every value of it; messages name it ~BT~Type.law~BT~.

        ~CODE~hive
        type Booking \{
        	room: Str
        	checkInDay: Int
        	checkOutDay: Int

        	law atLeastOneNight(b: Booking) \{
        		assert b.checkInDay >= 0 && b.checkInDay < b.checkOutDay
        	}

        }
        ~CODE~

        - **Each construction and each write must keep it on its own**: ~BT~T(...)~BT~, ~BT~x.f = e~BT~, a write further down a path, ~BT~append~BT~, ~BT~drop~BT~, ~BT~sort~BT~ or ~BT~hive.map.set~BT~ on a field, a field handed to a ~BT~mut~BT~ parameter. Change fields that move together as one new value: ~BT~b = Booking(b.room, 10, 12)~BT~.
        - **A ~BT~mut~BT~ sharing part of a lawful value may not write it**: after ~BT~mut lines := o.lines~BT~ (~BT~o~BT~ a ~BT~mut~BT~), ~BT~append(lines, x)~BT~ fails. → write ~BT~o.lines~BT~.
        - **The proof uses what the function knows there**: guards, early returns, bindings, ~BT~assert~BT~s passed, laws of values it holds; a call's answer is known only through its type's laws. A refusal names the law (~BT~Booking.atLeastOneNight~BT~), what is missing and what is known: guard it so the law holds there.
        - **Every value read keeps its laws.** Function laws rely on them, and the [bounds](bounds.md) pass takes each ~BT~assert~BT~ at the top of a type's law as a guard: under ~BT~assert p.rooms bounds p.selected~BT~, ~BT~p.rooms[p.selected]~BT~ needs none.
        - **Decoders check them at run time**, refusing a breaking value as ~BT~Broken~BT~, a variant of ~BT~JsonError~BT~, ~BT~JwtError~BT~, ~BT~QueryParamsError~BT~, ~BT~FlagError~BT~ and ~BT~SqlError~BT~.

        ## A table's laws

        A law ~BT~for table <name>~BT~ takes **one parameter**, the value a query runs for; its body is **a SQL filter**, read like a query body (Hive only in ~BT~\{…}~BT~ values and ~BT~if~BT~ conditions).

        ~CODE~hive
        law ownChatsOnly(contact: Contact) for table chats \{
        	fk_contact_from_id = \{contact.id} or
        	fk_contact_to_id = \{contact.id}
        }
        ~CODE~

        A body opening with ~BT~if~BT~, ~BT~or~BT~ or ~BT~and~BT~ is a ~BT~WHERE \{ }~BT~ block, applying where its ~BT~if~BT~s hold (~BT~or \{ }~BT~ blocks are refused); any other body always applies. **Every ~BT~query~BT~ touching the table takes a parameter of the law's type**, and each statement must:

        - **filter what it reads, changes or deletes**: an ANDed part of its WHERE, or of the ON of an inner or left JOIN bringing the table in, is the filter or says more (~BT~fk_contact_from_id = \{contact.id}~BT~ alone carries the law above); under a ~BT~WHERE \{ }~BT~ block's ~BT~if~BT~, where that condition follows from the law's.
        - **insert inside it**: write, into one of a part's ~BT~column = value~BT~ alternatives, that value; a part not of the form ~BT~column = value~BT~ refuses inserts.
        - **keep updates inside it**: SET a filtered column to the filter's value, or leave alone an alternative the WHERE guaranteed.

        A column is the table's when named through its alias, or bare in a statement reading that table alone. Subqueries, ~BT~WITH~BT~ and each ~BT~UNION~BT~ side carry the filter on their own. **~BT~hive.sql.unsafe~BT~ is held to no law.**

        ## How it is proved

        Stages in order, first to settle wins: **edge values** (~BT~0~BT~, ~BT~±1~BT~, ~BT~MIN~BT~, ~BT~MAX~BT~, empty and short vectors, every variant); **every value** where types are small; **unknowns**, split into cases wherever something looks inside one; **induction** on each vector parameter. Stages count steps, not time, so results match on every machine. ~BT~check~BT~, ~BT~build~BT~, ~BT~run~BT~ and ~BT~test~BT~ prove the laws of every file the entrypoint reaches.

        - **Integer ~BT~!=~BT~ is the two cases either side**: ~BT~n != 0~BT~ beside ~BT~n >= 0~BT~ shows ~BT~n > 0~BT~, in a law and where a type's law is kept.
        - **~BT~x / k~BT~ and ~BT~x % k~BT~ by a positive literal are tied to ~BT~x~BT~** (Go's truncating division): ~BT~n % 2 == 1~BT~ shows ~BT~0 <= n / 2 < n~BT~.
        - **~BT~indexOf~BT~ on a vector is followed through each element it is known to hold**, splitting where one may or may not match: ~BT~indexOf([], x)~BT~ is an error for any ~BT~x~BT~; ~BT~indexOf(["*", ...rest], "*")~BT~ finds it. Past those, in a vector of unknown length, it is one unknown ~BT~Result~BT~ per vector and value.
        - **A call with no ~BT~mut~BT~ parameter answers as before on that path**: under ~BT~if unique(pages)~BT~, a type law asking ~BT~unique(u.pages)~BT~ of the value built there knows it. A call on known values is followed to its end.

        **A generic law is proved per instantiation**, at every set of types the program uses the generic callables it calls at; one nothing instantiates is not checked.

        *From ~BT~spec/19-laws.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

func cli(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # The ~BT~hive~BT~ command line

        ~CODE~
        hive check     <entrypoint.hive>       Report any errors, build nothing
        hive emit      <entrypoint.hive>       Print the generated Go source
        hive build     <entrypoint.hive>       Compile to a native executable
        hive export    <entrypoint.hive>       Package as an app, for a handset
        hive run       <entrypoint.hive> [..]  Compile and run
        hive install   <entrypoint.hive>       Build it and put it where a shell finds commands
        hive test      <entrypoint.hive>       Run the program's tests, with coverage
        hive test      <directory>             Run every test under it, git-ignored files aside
        hive analyze   <entrypoint.hive>       Score what it will cost, and write a page
        hive container <entrypoint.hive>       Write a Dockerfile that builds and runs it
        hive agents                            Write .hivedocs/ for coding agents
        hive indent    <directory or file>     Indent every Hive file there with spaces
        hive lsp                               Answer an editor, as a language server
        hive use       <version>               Replace this compiler with another release
        hive version                           Print which release this compiler is

          --target <goos>/<goarch>             Build for another platform: on ~BT~build~BT~,
                                               ~BT~export~BT~, and ~BT~install~BT~ (android/arm64)
          --name <name>                        What ~BT~install~BT~ puts the program in as
        ~CODE~

        The entrypoint is the file holding ~BT~proc main(): void~BT~; a program is that file plus everything it imports.

        - **~BT~check~BT~** runs every check, stops before any Go is written, and writes nothing beside the file. **Run it after every edit.**
        - **~BT~test~BT~** **exits non-zero when any test fails**.
        - **~BT~run~BT~** passes everything after the entrypoint to the program, chdirs to the entrypoint's folder, and exits with the program's status.
        - **~BT~build~BT~** puts the executable beside the entrypoint and the generated Go module in ~BT~<entrypoint>-build/~BT~.
        - **~BT~emit~BT~** prints Go to stdout (~BT~hive emit x.hive > main.go~BT~), progress and errors to stderr.

        ~BT~go tool dist list~BT~ names every platform ~BT~build~BT~ takes. An unknown flag is **refused, not dropped**, as is anything after the entrypoint of a command that takes nothing more. A compile error starts ~BT~file:line:~BT~ (the imported file it happened in), a usage error ~BT~hive:~BT~; both exit non-zero.

        ## Diagnostics

        ~CODE~
        main.hive:12: cannot prove this index is in range: the index is a computed
            expression. Bind it to a variable and guard it (~BT~if j >= 0 && j < len(v)~BT~).
        ~CODE~

        Nothing precedes ~BT~file:line: message~BT~; continuation lines are **indented**, so one error is one quickfix entry. Vim or Neovim:

        ~CODE~vim
        setlocal makeprg=hive\ check\ %:S
        setlocal errorformat=%-G\ %.%#,%f:%l:\ %m,%-G%.%#
        ~CODE~

        Every error names the file and line it is on, in an imported module too.

        ## ~BT~hive export~BT~

        ~CODE~
        hive export chat.hive --target android/arm64     writes chat-android-arm64.apk
        ~CODE~

        The ~BT~hive build~BT~ program behind a WebView host, signed and installable. **No Android SDK or network**: the build writes the manifest, resource table and APK Signature Scheme v2 signature, keyed by ~BT~~/.hive/android.key~BT~ (made on first use, so later builds install over earlier ones).

        - Application id from the entrypoint: ~BT~chat.hive~BT~ is ~BT~hive.chat~BT~. Launcher icon: ~BT~assets/icon.png~BT~.
        - ~BT~android/arm64~BT~ is the only Android target. A program that opens no window is refused.
        - Permissions ~BT~INTERNET~BT~ and ~BT~ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE~BT~. ~BT~$HOME~BT~ and working directory are the app's files directory; output goes to logcat.
        - Runs with ~BT~GOGC=off~BT~ and ~BT~GOMEMLIMIT=256MiB~BT~. Only intent extras ~BT~HIVE_FOLDS~BT~, ~BT~GOGC~BT~ and ~BT~GOMEMLIMIT~BT~ reach the environment; ~BT~HIVE_INSPECT~BT~ lets ~BT~chrome://inspect~BT~ attach:

        ~CODE~
        adb shell am start -n hive.chat/dev.hive.app.MainActivity --es HIVE_FOLDS 1
        ~CODE~

        ## ~BT~hive analyze~BT~

        Takes an entrypoint, no flags; builds and runs nothing, so it is about as fast as ~BT~check~BT~. Scores every ~BT~proc~BT~ and ~BT~func~BT~ out of 100, prints the worst, writes ~BT~<entrypoint>-analysis.html~BT~ beside the entrypoint:

        ~CODE~
          chat.hive scores 93/100 over 37 callables
          8 findings — 7 copies to pay for, 1 wait inside a loop

          50  greet                               proc, line 512
                ×8     hive.task.sleep(500)
                     ~BT~hive.task.sleep~BT~ waits on the clock, once per turn of the loop
        ~CODE~

        Findings: a loop in a loop, a linear search in one, a name rebuilt from itself each turn, a call in a loop handed nothing the loop changes, a wait per turn on a disk, network, database or another process, and an **implicit deep copy**. A copy finding is exact (the emitter will emit it, at about that size); **every other finding is a weight**, multiplied by a constant inside two loops, not by a length. The score is logarithmic, ten points per doubling of cost; nothing to report scores 100.

        **It also lists what is never reached** (every ~BT~func~BT~, ~BT~proc~BT~, ~BT~query~BT~ and type nothing reaches from ~BT~main~BT~ or a test, however indirectly) and every parameter its body never reads:

        ~CODE~
          2 declarations never reached from ~BT~main~BT~:
                cart.oldTotal                       func, line 40
                cart.legacyTax                      func, line 52 — named only by cart.oldTotal

          1 parameter never read:
                cart.price                          ~BT~currency~BT~, line 18
        ~CODE~

        Declarations named only by unreached code are listed beside what names them. Skipped: parameters starting with ~BT~_~BT~, and those of a callable passed as a value. A file with neither ~BT~main~BT~ nor a test lists nothing.

        ## ~BT~hive container~BT~

        Writes ~BT~Dockerfile~BT~ in the current folder, or ~BT~Dockerfile-hive-container~BT~ if one exists, and says which. A program that does not compile gets its errors, as from ~BT~hive check~BT~.

        ~CODE~
        hive container main.hive
        docker build -t main .
        docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 main
        ~CODE~

        Only Docker is needed. Stage one downloads Go and **the compiler that wrote the file** (~BT~ARG HIVEC_VERSION~BT~), so the image never moves to a newer release on its own; stage two is the executable alone on ~BT~distroless/static~BT~. ~BT~hive.net.httpServe(8080, ...)~BT~ becomes ~BT~EXPOSE 8080~BT~; an import naming a repository adds ~BT~git~BT~ to the build stage.

        ## ~BT~hive agents~BT~

        Writes this documentation; no entrypoint, no flags. Rewrites its own files under ~BT~.hivedocs/~BT~ from the running compiler, leaving others alone, plus ~BT~.hivedocs/plugin/~BT~, a Claude Code plugin starting the language server. Creates ~BT~AGENTS.md~BT~ if missing, adds ~BT~.hivedocs/~BT~ to an existing ~BT~.gitignore~BT~, and puts ~BT~@AGENTS.md~BT~ atop a ~BT~CLAUDE.md~BT~ not opening with it. **Rerun it after upgrading the compiler.**

        ## ~BT~hive indent~BT~

        Reindents with spaces every Hive file under a directory (git-ignored files skipped), or one file: a tab moves to the next multiple of four. Names each file changed.

        - **Literal contents never change**: a multi-line ~BT~"..."~BT~ string keeps every character; a backtick string or query body loses tabs only in the indentation the compiler strips anyway.
        - A file that does not lex is left alone and named; the command exits non-zero.
        - Its only added line is an empty one around a type's law: a law never touches a field, a variant or the type's braces (two laws may touch). Comment lines right above a law move with it.

        ## ~BT~hive lsp~BT~

        A language server on standard input and output: **what is wrong** while typing, **what a name is**, **where it was declared**, **what could come next** (a value's fields, a module's calls, a type's variants, names in scope), the file's outline, and the file reindented as ~BT~hive indent~BT~ would. It reads a file as the editor holds it, saved or not, as a program of its own: no entrypoint, no project setup. See [lsp](lsp.md).

        ## ~BT~hive install~BT~

        ~BT~hive install tool.hive~BT~ builds as ~BT~hive build~BT~ does and moves the program to ~BT~~/.local/bin~BT~ (Linux, macOS) or ~BT~%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Hive\bin~BT~ (Windows) to run by name. Reinstalling replaces it, even while it runs.

        | flag | effect |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~--name notes~BT~ | installs as ~BT~notes~BT~ (~BT~notes.exe~BT~ on Windows), not the entrypoint's name |
        | ~BT~--desktop~BT~ | also adds a windowed program to the app launcher, icon ~BT~assets/icon.png~BT~ if shipped: a ~BT~.desktop~BT~ entry in ~BT~~/.local/share/applications~BT~ (Linux), an app in ~BT~~/Applications~BT~ (macOS), a Start Menu shortcut (Windows). ~BT~--name~BT~ may contain spaces. A program with no window is refused. |
        | ~BT~--target android/arm64~BT~ | packages as ~BT~hive export~BT~ does and installs through ~BT~adb~BT~ on the USB-connected phone (USB debugging on). Reinstalling replaces the app. |

        Windows: the folder goes on ~BT~PATH~BT~ the first time; reopen older terminals. Elsewhere, if ~BT~~/.local/bin~BT~ is not on ~BT~PATH~BT~, ~BT~hive install~BT~ says what to add to the shell's startup file.

        ## ~BT~hive use~BT~

        ~BT~hive use v0.3.4~BT~ downloads that release's compiler for this platform, checks it against the release's ~BT~SHA256SUMS~BT~, and puts it where the running one is. From **v0.5.0**, ~BT~SHA256SUMS~BT~ must carry the release key's signature or nothing is replaced; earlier releases are unsigned. The ~BT~v~BT~ is optional; the oldest reachable is **v0.3.3**. On Windows the replaced compiler stays beside the new one, its name ending in ~BT~~~BT~.

        *From ~BT~spec/17-diagnostics.md~BT~ and the compiler's own ~BT~src/hivec.hive~BT~ and
        ~BT~src/analyze.hive~BT~.*
    `)
}

func lsp(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # The language server

        ~BT~hive lsp~BT~ answers questions about a program without building it, reading files as they stand, saved or not. **If your harness has a language-server tool, ask it before reading a file**: an answer costs a line of context, the file costs the file.

        | to learn | ask for | instead of |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | what a name is: a variable's type, a call's signature, a field, a variant | **hover** on the name | opening the file that declares it |
        | where a name was declared | **go to definition** | searching for it |
        | what a file declares | **document symbols** | reading the file |

        A hover answers in Hive: a call's whole signature (argument names and order), library calls included; a variable's ~BT~name: Type~BT~; a type's declaration; what a field holds. Names inside ~BT~"...\{name}..."~BT~ strings count.

        ~CODE~hive
        func hive.term.exec(command: Str, arguments: Str[dyn], directory: Str): Result<Str, ExecError>
        ~CODE~

        On every change it reports, with line and column, the errors ~BT~hive check~BT~ prints; read them before going on. **It reads one file as a program of its own**, with its imports but not the rest of the project, so ~BT~hive check <entrypoint.hive>~BT~ still decides whether an edit is right.

        **Refused:** references, workspace symbols, implementations, call hierarchies, rename; search instead. Names inside a backtick string or a ~BT~query~BT~ body get no answer.

        ## Connecting it

        ~CODE~
        command   hive lsp     over standard input and output
        files     *.hive       language id: hive
        ~CODE~

        **Claude Code** loads it from the plugin ~BT~hive agents~BT~ writes beside these pages:

        ~CODE~
        claude --plugin-dir .hivedocs/plugin
        ~CODE~

        **In an editor**, an agent has the editor's setup: ~BT~LSP.md~BT~ in the Hive repository covers VS Code and Neovim.

        If your tool finds no server for a ~BT~.hive~BT~ file, it is not connected: tell whoever you work for, quoting the line above, instead of going on without it.

        *From ~BT~LSP.md~BT~ and ~BT~spec/17-diagnostics.md~BT~ in the Hive repository.*
    `)
}

func pitfalls(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # Pitfalls

        All are **compile errors**; → is the fix.

        ## Conditions and operators

        - **A condition must be a ~BT~Bool~BT~**: no truthiness for an ~BT~Atom~BT~, ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Str~BT~ or vector. → ~BT~if flag == #Ready~BT~, ~BT~if len(v) > 0~BT~, ~BT~if s != ""~BT~.
        - **~BT~!~BT~ takes only a ~BT~Bool~BT~.**
        - **~BT~!~BT~ binds tight** (like unary ~BT~-~BT~): ~BT~!a && b~BT~ is ~BT~(!a) && b~BT~; ~BT~!x is Shape.Point~BT~ is ~BT~(!x) is …~BT~, a type error. → ~BT~!(a == b)~BT~, ~BT~!(v bounds i)~BT~.
        - **A match cannot be negated**: ~BT~!(x is Shape.Circle(r))~BT~. → put the other case in the ~BT~else~BT~.
        - **No number is widened for you**: ~BT~hive.math.max(0, health)~BT~ fails (it takes ~BT~Float~BT~). → ~BT~hive.conv.itf(0)~BT~.
        - **Comparing two different types** is an error, not ~BT~false~BT~.

        ## Vectors

        - **~BT~[dyn]~BT~ is declared, never inferred**: ~BT~mut v := ["a"]~BT~ is a ~BT~Str[1]~BT~ and cannot be ~BT~append~BT~ed to. → ~BT~mut Str[dyn] v = ["a"]~BT~.
        - **~BT~append~BT~, ~BT~prepend~BT~ and ~BT~drop~BT~ need a ~BT~mut~BT~ target.**
        - **Every index must be proved**: computed ~BT~v[len(v) - 1]~BT~ is refused. → ~BT~last := len(v) - 1~BT~, then ~BT~if v bounds last \{ v[last] }~BT~.
        - **A slice's high bound is inclusive**: ~BT~v[0:2]~BT~ is **three** elements.
        - **~BT~filter~BT~ and ~BT~filterMap~BT~ lose the length**: indexing their result always needs a guard.
        - **~BT~Str[]~BT~ is a parameter spelling only**; a return or storage says ~BT~[dyn]~BT~ or ~BT~[n]~BT~.
        - **A ~BT~[dyn]~BT~ parameter refuses a static vector**: ~BT~count([1, 2])~BT~ against ~BT~count(v: Int[dyn])~BT~. → ~BT~Int[]~BT~ if it only reads; declare the vector ~BT~[dyn]~BT~ if the callee grows it.

        ## Strings

        - **A ~BT~Str~BT~'s length is never known**: ~BT~s := "abc"~BT~ still needs ~BT~if s bounds 1~BT~ before ~BT~s[1]~BT~. An index from ~BT~indexOf~BT~ needs no guard.
        - **~BT~s[0] = "x"~BT~ fails**: a ~BT~Str~BT~ is a value. Build a new one.
        - **~BT~Str~BT~ indexing counts characters, not bytes**, yielding a one-character ~BT~Str~BT~. ~BT~len~BT~ counts characters, ~BT~bytes~BT~ bytes.

        ## Names and declarations

        - **A keyword's spelling is never a name**: no field ~BT~type~BT~. → ~BT~kind: Str -- JSON as type;~BT~. Other casings are free (~BT~Return~BT~ is a fine variant).
        - **A ~BT~mut~BT~ variable may not be ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~**, loop counters included. An immutable one may be, never must: ~BT~camelCase~BT~ is the default for every variable.
        - **~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~ is for variables only**: parameters, fields, callables, import names, and pattern or ~BT~for each~BT~ bindings are ~BT~camelCase~BT~.
        - **A ~BT~func~BT~ cannot declare a ~BT~mut~BT~ parameter.** → use a ~BT~proc~BT~. A ~BT~func~BT~ may still call a ~BT~proc~BT~.
        - **Every non-~BT~void~BT~ path must return**; an ~BT~if~BT~/~BT~else if~BT~ chain over a whole union counts, as does a ~BT~switch~BT~ whose every arm returns.
        - **~BT~mut~BT~ goes only on a declaration or a ~BT~proc~BT~ parameter**: never on a field, a return, or in a function type.

        ## Matching

        - **A ~BT~switch~BT~ never falls through and must cover every case**: one position per subject in each arm, first match runs; an uncovered value or an arm that can never run is an error.
        - **A pattern left of ~BT~:=~BT~ needs an ~BT~else~BT~ whose every arm leaves** (~BT~return~BT~, ~BT~panic~BT~, ~BT~break~BT~, ~BT~continue~BT~). → ~BT~Result.Ok(n) := parse(s) else \{ Result.Error(why) \{ return Result.Error(why) } }~BT~.
        - **Name every field of a variant or none**; ~BT~_~BT~ for unwanted ones.
        - **A variant is built by calling it**, even an empty one: ~BT~Shape.Point~BT~ names the variant, not a value. → ~BT~Shape.Point()~BT~; ~BT~Shape.Circle(_)~BT~ for a function value.
        - **String patterns never make a chain exhaustive** (except a lone ~BT~"\{s}"~BT~): end with an ~BT~else~BT~ or a ~BT~return~BT~.
        - **Two open string-pattern holes may not be adjacent** (~BT~"\{a}\{b}"~BT~). → a literal between them, or a regex on one.
        - **An ~BT~is~BT~ binding is out of scope in the ~BT~else~BT~.**

        ## Concurrency

        - **~BT~async~BT~ is not an expression**: only a statement of its own or a declaration's value side.
        - **Every ~BT~await~BT~ entry must be a call**, all of one answer type; ~BT~await []~BT~ fails.
        - **A ~BT~void~BT~ call takes no ~BT~async~BT~ binding and no ~BT~with timeout~BT~.**
        - **An ~BT~async~BT~ call gets a copy of a ~BT~mut~BT~ argument**, not the caller's storage.
        - **One call may not lend a vector and a path inside it**: ~BT~both(v, v[0])~BT~ with both parameters ~BT~mut~BT~. → lend ~BT~v~BT~; reach ~BT~v[0]~BT~ through it.
        - **An address states what its service takes**: a bare ~BT~Address~BT~ fails, as does ~BT~box := at(#Cache)~BT~ unless something later sends to ~BT~box~BT~ or hands it to a parameter. → ~BT~Address<Op> box = at(#Cache)~BT~, or a send: ~BT~box(Op.Ping())~BT~.
        - **A library error is a union variant.** → ~BT~why is file.FileError.NotFound~BT~, or a ~BT~switch~BT~ over every one.

        ## Build time

        - **~BT~with compiler~BT~ goes in the entrypoint only**; an imported file runs nothing at build time. → call it there and hand the answer down.
        - **Its arguments must be known before the program runs**: literals, vectors and constructors of them, answers of other such calls. ~BT~n := 2~BT~ then ~BT~f(n) with compiler~BT~ fails. → ~BT~f(2) with compiler~BT~, or ~BT~n := two() with compiler~BT~.
        - **Its answer must be data**: not a function, an ~BT~Address~BT~, a ~BT~Secret~BT~, a library-owned type such as ~BT~hive.json.JsonValue~BT~, or nothing.

        ## Collections and modules

        - **~BT~hive.map.new()~BT~ needs what it holds stated**, where it lands or by later use; ~BT~m := hive.map.new()~BT~ alone fails. → ~BT~hive.map.set(m, "a", 1)~BT~, or ~BT~mut hive.map.Map<Str, Int> m = hive.map.new()~BT~.
        - **A number never changes size on its own**: ~BT~UInt8 b = n~BT~ (~BT~n~BT~ an ~BT~Int~BT~), ~BT~Int n = b~BT~, ~BT~UInt4 x = 16~BT~ fail. → ~BT~UInt8(n)~BT~ keeps the low bits, ~BT~UInt8.checked(n)~BT~ gives a ~BT~Result~BT~, ~BT~Int(b)~BT~ widens.
        - **A map has no subscript and no ~BT~for each~BT~.** → ~BT~hive.map.get~BT~; loop over ~BT~hive.map.keys(m)~BT~.
        - **A Hive import never writes ~BT~.hive~BT~; a Go import always writes ~BT~.go~BT~.**
        - **No import may be named ~BT~hive~BT~**, and there is no ~BT~import hive~BT~: the library is reached a module at a time.

        ## Laws

        - **~BT~law~BT~ is a keyword, ~BT~axiom~BT~ reserved**: neither names a variable, parameter or field. → ~BT~rule~BT~, ~BT~statute~BT~, whatever the code means.
        - **Only a law may call a law**; no law is a value, and nothing runs one.
        - **A law calling itself passes something smaller**: a part a pattern took from that parameter, ~BT~if xs is [_, ...rest] \{ itself(rest) }~BT~. Laws may not call each other in a circle.
        - **A law may not reach a loop other than ~BT~for each~BT~ or a trusted counting loop, or a non-shrinking recursion**; the error names the path.
        - **~BT~a + 1 > a~BT~ is not a law**: ~BT~Int~BT~ wraps; the compiler finds ~BT~a = MAX~BT~.

        ## Generics and values

        - **A generic callable, a builtin or a library call is not a value**, bare or partially applied.
        - **A type variable only in the return type** cannot be inferred.

        ## Queries

        - **No ~BT~SELECT *~BT~ against a declared row type.** Columns match fields **by name**: alias any spelled differently.
        - **A column name or sort direction is never a ~BT~\{param}~BT~.**

        ## Sorting

        - **~BT~b := sort(a)~BT~ leaves ~BT~a~BT~ unsorted** and answers a new vector. Only a **discarded** ~BT~sort~BT~ on a ~BT~mut~BT~ vector sorts in place; a discarded one that cannot is an error.

        *Every rule here is stated in full on the page for its feature; start at
        [README](README.md).*
    `)
}
